[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c9367d1-0f85-5aa9-8314-82e23cfcb4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376159} {:answer/id #uuid "05545f34-328e-5921-bcb6-15d83fd12d46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376160} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3641ab-3aee-5bb3-b85d-42c2c0544f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376161} {:answer/id #uuid "59ebe1de-4988-54c8-aa0f-503ed2400462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376162} {:answer/id #uuid "dece23e5-12fd-5d7c-96ca-bc522e2f38eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dece23e5-12fd-5d7c-96ca-bc522e2f38eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376163} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3683d5d-d89b-541a-93b0-9dd5127f3765" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10dc60fb-f25e-52f7-9f87-59ad72f93d31" :question/math-academy-id "q-174392" :question/problem "$$\n{1}_{A}(c)=0,\\,{1}_{B}(c)=1\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(c)=1$\n- ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(c)=0$\n- $c\\in A\\cap B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(c) & =min{{1}_{A}(c),{1}_{B}(c)} \\\\  & =min{0,1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(c)=0,$ we conclude that $c$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203911 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5725601-8181-582a-935d-d3dfde851d63" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functional Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56dc2b91-fa08-52f1-b205-fc89397886b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376709} {:answer/id #uuid "d19dbafd-3cd5-5f7c-a71b-f6d0068ca02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $S$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376710} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a312b3-8689-58cc-8ccb-dc7b547a534a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376711} {:answer/id #uuid "87b475a8-324e-5f0d-bfb1-9f13d14a996e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376712} {:answer/id #uuid "353fc6d0-0627-5629-9d53-796e92699492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56dc2b91-fa08-52f1-b205-fc89397886b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376709} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d458a17-ac4e-55a1-8937-54fa569d2d12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd2e7ad1-df50-507b-b322-c68735f660cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-216886" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are functional?\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x={y}^{2},$ defined on $R$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x{y}^{3}=1,$ defined on ${R}^{+}$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $y∣2x,$ defined on $Z$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined over $A$ and $B$ is *functional* (or a *partial function*) if, for all $a\\in A$ and ${b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\in B,$ we have \n\n$$\n(a,{b}_{1})\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(a,{b}_{2})\\in R\\,⇒\\,{b}_{1}={b}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the first components of two ordered pairs are equal, then the second components must also be equal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- $R$ is *not* functional. Notice that $1={1}^{2}$ and $1=(-1)^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains pairs $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ with the same first component but different second components.\n\n- $S$ is functional. Indeed, $a{b}_{1}^{3}=1$ and $a{b}_{2}^{3}=1$ is equivalent to ${b}_{1}={b}_{2}$ over ${R}^{+}.$ So, when the first components of two pairs are equal, then the second components are also equal.\n\n- $T$ is *not* functional. Notice that $2∣2(3)$ and $3∣2(3).$ So, $T$ contains pairs $(3,2)$ and $(3,3)$ with the same first component but different second components.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "696e584f-f454-593e-bd58-abb69e57b3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377650} {:answer/id #uuid "a3452674-e7ae-5e7f-858b-9637d1e75a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377651} {:answer/id #uuid "a978c7d2-6987-586e-9304-4ae71f2c36a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377652} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c0e42c-4f23-58b3-8d95-773d2a6c79d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377653} {:answer/id #uuid "a27fb0f0-ebd3-5673-9103-2976012540f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a27fb0f0-ebd3-5673-9103-2976012540f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377654} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a35593-4c9e-5037-b87f-d4e48a3bf713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10cee0d6-c023-521a-a779-cab7a62d3b8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64999" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7ffcda47f0d750ce9f25e50b0b28091ff4279467313fbe604eac0fc9b1229806.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/08/080d6cf1cd468b7ddfe65df2e6a21792e1afe470ddd571c6b5ec25569d1fb537.png)\n\n- Statement I is false. Every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to \n$M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $A$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ecef2a6-800e-52b8-9b40-582617d37a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377657} {:answer/id #uuid "799ce251-6650-5be0-bd9c-daa670e00626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377658} {:answer/id #uuid "bd40ecaa-5223-545f-b4c8-8788036c9fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377659} {:answer/id #uuid "886e9c56-b053-5c0a-8f91-67e86c3a1d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377660} {:answer/id #uuid "77cacc5d-e24f-51c9-b340-6e624eb8e78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "799ce251-6650-5be0-bd9c-daa670e00626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377658} :answer-field/id #uuid "c485ba5f-5125-5c6b-bc5a-42f3171d651d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afb4c2fe-2048-5475-a89e-bbe7b7062429" :question/math-academy-id "q-71689" :question/problem "![](images/94/94911d7be45c5572a4ce47ce95c81a66bf6276144eeace8dd74cac15646c2082.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/50/5024d8cf4c7a21667eb91dadee2d3368f1f9f3504e2a06f27b020c2d18804586.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $C$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cedd946a-0499-50fc-ba16-0a833e8d00a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377670} {:answer/id #uuid "f806dacb-99da-5f7d-b073-bed3efc36928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377671} {:answer/id #uuid "fe56c20a-0f38-5c7b-a3fb-f5a9b0a7c7da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377672} {:answer/id #uuid "c4acc27d-db09-5869-a82d-b968a4088aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377673} {:answer/id #uuid "318375fd-a989-5bb5-b37b-42a61428b01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "318375fd-a989-5bb5-b37b-42a61428b01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377674} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a89f9af-28e6-56d8-9d34-9b6196cc4f17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aafb0544-5c12-5527-bc2a-2417215aad99" :question/math-academy-id "q-73566" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is **not** invertible\n- The lines $2x-3y=1$ and $2x-2y=2$ intersect at exactly one point\n- $Ax=0$ has no solution" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false since, according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a7cc05b-4143-5191-a5e9-d4ab94e09a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377765} {:answer/id #uuid "d0184665-f93e-563e-82f8-842a76f9bd8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377766} {:answer/id #uuid "590f4fb6-6508-551b-9c11-b1551e26a497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377767} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9bc119-5748-526f-9158-ef10267d3396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377768} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd0dd26-4f04-5458-99f5-876504ceec53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bd0dd26-4f04-5458-99f5-876504ceec53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377769} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef75295-8f00-53ba-bcd0-1da090f1e272" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c7ff497-8845-5d34-a5a1-e0b10d7a1e25" :question/math-academy-id "q-79367" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix with $3$ pivot columns?\n\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- $A$ is not  invertible\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=5.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e321ab72-a38c-5bc3-a372-36d4637ca766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377786} {:answer/id #uuid "73ca614a-609c-5789-8216-e1d64d97ef21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377787} {:answer/id #uuid "8b389f97-7280-58d9-a867-bbfe0c48f6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377788} {:answer/id #uuid "ff487218-911e-5dce-bce2-7c8b8a382610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377789} {:answer/id #uuid "09089628-1b96-5010-b46a-0050369c47f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73ca614a-609c-5789-8216-e1d64d97ef21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377787} :answer-field/id #uuid "a683c554-aa84-5585-a029-cfe23dcd9c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e6dc3a9-8bf9-5ff1-b677-65bc92bbe439" :question/math-academy-id "q-81483" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\n- $¬(A∧¬B)$\n- $¬A∧B$\n- $¬A∨B$" :question/worked-solution "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬(A∧¬B)\\,$ | $\\,¬A∧B\\,$ | $\\,¬A∨B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬(A∧¬B)$ and $¬A∨B$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aab3427-9f2a-51eb-8b32-2399be1df3a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377793} {:answer/id #uuid "1c57abd5-b45e-52d7-bc08-2cdd35200def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377794} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab5b326-5c13-5171-980a-ce3a0a797243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377795} {:answer/id #uuid "4b48a152-5981-51ed-a42e-233a6539fd7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377796} {:answer/id #uuid "0493020b-721c-5d41-821f-7d1c65468cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c57abd5-b45e-52d7-bc08-2cdd35200def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377794} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc8c3d25-f65a-5900-af29-d2774b621a26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6020d90d-05af-58b3-8eac-fa53d62d19da" :question/math-academy-id "q-81486" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\n- $¬(X∧¬Y)$\n- $¬(X∨¬Y)$\n- $¬X∧Y$" :question/worked-solution "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,X\\,$ | $\\,Y\\,$ | $\\,¬(X∧¬Y)\\,$ | $\\,¬(X∨¬Y)\\,$ | $\\,¬X∧Y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬(X∨¬Y)$ and $¬X∧Y$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64a3922f-7e74-562f-9bb9-c4a41e912ce7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e191b3-f6c6-5184-a891-5fac4093e93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377867} {:answer/id #uuid "bad99892-543e-56ad-9a6e-856af720ea78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377868} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0d9e17-8b7a-5383-8c55-d8ec8cc4db17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377869} {:answer/id #uuid "6d28f4e9-e6fd-58bd-b645-eebd59576eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377870} {:answer/id #uuid "5266792f-9254-59ee-9dbc-ae0292af8b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13e191b3-f6c6-5184-a891-5fac4093e93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377867} :answer-field/id #uuid "88539d75-c77a-55a8-9e2f-8812818cf2d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28c545f7-d207-5c17-a488-3a5f17bee359" :question/math-academy-id "q-82071" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- **If** $2$ is an even number, **then** $8$ is a prime number.\n- **If** $8$ is an even number, **then** $2$ is an even number.\n- **If** $8$ is a prime number, **then** $2$ is an even number." :question/worked-solution "An implication $A⇒B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. In other words, the implication is true unless the \"promise\" is broken.\n\n- Statement I is false. The antecedent \"$2$ is an even number\" is true, and the consequent \"$8$ is a prime number\" is false. So the promise is broken, and the statement is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. The antecedent \"$8$ is an even number\" is true, and the consequent \"$2$ is an even number\" is true. So the promise is kept, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. The antecedent \"$8$ is a prime number\" is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64a3922f-7e74-562f-9bb9-c4a41e912ce7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54410334-c0b4-5c38-b6ef-b5b149ae8cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377874} {:answer/id #uuid "024a7241-357d-53a4-9ef4-0f4cb3c4f4d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377875} {:answer/id #uuid "8a84a82e-bb55-5b82-8bb2-1c35c90f74c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377876} {:answer/id #uuid "b31e411c-bb65-5445-b25f-ea0bc7446909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377877} {:answer/id #uuid "02861e96-1e67-5c16-8c96-a619c64d8c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54410334-c0b4-5c38-b6ef-b5b149ae8cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377874} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6874c49-faf5-50d4-9433-56a0523e843e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd2bb687-e9d9-5998-a0f1-5d9b7cc6698c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82088" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- **If** $7$ is divisible by $2$, **then** $8$ is divisible by $4.$\n- **If** $8$ is divisible by $4$, **then** $7$ is divisible by $2.$\n- **If** $7$ is divisible by $4$, **then** $8$ is divisible by $2.$" :question/worked-solution "An implication $A⇒B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. In other words, the implication is true unless the \"promise\" is broken.\n\n- Statement I is true. The antecedent \"$7$ is divisible by $2$\" is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. The antecedent \"$8$ is divisible by $4$\" is true, and the consequent \"$7$ is divisible by $2$\" is false. So the promise is broken, and the statement is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Again, the antecedent \"$7$ is divisible by $4$ is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64a3922f-7e74-562f-9bb9-c4a41e912ce7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f471ffe-39d9-5cca-af20-8c094c9dcf4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377881} {:answer/id #uuid "2f25dc99-ae72-5fee-9701-6c5422bb460f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377882} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e8e06c-d3f8-5d2f-853a-20ce61ba063b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377883} {:answer/id #uuid "d26a22a7-a8ba-5f39-b63c-1351f792d5f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377884} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea80694-1e35-5138-9aa9-14406644ce5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f471ffe-39d9-5cca-af20-8c094c9dcf4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377881} :answer-field/id #uuid "61303d7f-a5cf-53cf-80eb-bc0761d300ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e834c31-c35d-57df-a0ce-02370148728a" :question/math-academy-id "q-82089" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- **If** $2+3<4,$ **then** $1+5<8.$\n- **If** $1+5<8,$ **then** $2+3<4.$\n- **If** $1+5<4,$ **then** $2+3<8.$" :question/worked-solution "An implication $A⇒B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. In other words, the implication is true unless the \"promise\" is broken.\n\n- Statement I is true. The antecedent $2+3<4$ is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. The antecedent $1+5<8$ is true, and the consequent $2+3<4$ is false. So the promise is broken, and the statement is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Again, the antecedent $1+5<4$ is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "281873c8-e173-5181-8281-96b84d636b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Chinese Remainder Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa3016eb-291f-5ec0-bcf5-22ef16b8043f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377895} {:answer/id #uuid "14aed1ff-f3f2-56a3-8a5b-4aee72b089a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377896} {:answer/id #uuid "0c4be289-4fe7-51ce-badb-d5a9568ee976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377897} {:answer/id #uuid "a810b8fc-0777-5c13-bf21-17710dfd3e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377898} {:answer/id #uuid "12be2f6e-3c4c-5e44-b5f8-4e550f2e3648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12be2f6e-3c4c-5e44-b5f8-4e550f2e3648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377899} :answer-field/id #uuid "01ae11c9-f622-50bf-a7c7-2005925c8a44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b00b20e-2b69-5a9e-a153-226cf102bd50" :question/math-academy-id "q-83978" :question/problem "According to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $48?$ \n\n$$\n\\text{I.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{II.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{III.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system I since $6$ and $8$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(6,8)=2>1\n$$\n\n- The theorem applies to system II. The numbers $3,$ and $16$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $3\\cdot 16=48.$\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system III since $4$ and $12$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,12)=4>1\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f8227e2-8ccf-55e2-9bbf-51d1887099c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377971} {:answer/id #uuid "4446d93a-94d1-50c2-9cd8-bfbf3a04a0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377972} {:answer/id #uuid "d99e656c-bbf8-5fb3-866d-a3cc6e7277e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377973} {:answer/id #uuid "5f29f94c-82b2-53f0-84ba-db7dd6cd56c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377974} {:answer/id #uuid "73531dd9-bdee-5872-8c2d-ee0c25c8789e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4446d93a-94d1-50c2-9cd8-bfbf3a04a0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377972} :answer-field/id #uuid "39aa91b6-39c4-5bfc-86c0-4c65c4f7778f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4951a3e-3ef9-5319-bbf9-d2c571b3026e" :question/math-academy-id "q-86684" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\n- $¬(¬X∧¬Y)$\n- $¬X∨¬Y$\n- $¬(X∧Y)$" :question/worked-solution "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,X\\,$ | $\\,Y\\,$ | $\\,¬(¬X∧¬Y)\\,$ | $\\,¬X∨¬Y\\,$ | $\\,¬(X∧Y)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬X∨¬Y$ and $¬(X∧Y)$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc20167-567b-5eba-8c5e-d41b0bb593f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377978} {:answer/id #uuid "2fce39e2-0215-5f04-85bd-f5722d948c69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377979} {:answer/id #uuid "a707aa92-d9f7-5cd2-9eae-5036825cc2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377980} {:answer/id #uuid "b67a6a05-d7db-505f-94b7-b27bec775d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377981} {:answer/id #uuid "d5483f18-a3e5-5029-941e-4175e51381c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a707aa92-d9f7-5cd2-9eae-5036825cc2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377980} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bed9a28-ca77-5562-a870-aaf34e572c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17f91135-f9d8-56cf-88b5-eaf7c61a4e5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-86690" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\n- $¬X∧(X∨¬Y)$\n- $¬X∨(X∧¬Y)$\n- $¬(X∨(¬X∧Y))$" :question/worked-solution "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,X\\,$ | $\\,Y\\,$ | $\\,¬X∧(X∨¬Y)\\,$ | $\\,¬X∨(X∧¬Y)\\,$ | $\\,¬(X∨(¬X∧Y))\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬X∧(X∨¬Y)$ and $¬(X∨(¬X∧Y))$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4106fe70-b9df-5621-ba64-66703855aaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Conjunction Absorbs Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c286ba3-90a5-54e4-9eb4-c8a02089e4e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378183} {:answer/id #uuid "99f71640-1095-56b8-a3fd-7d5b129b057d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378184} {:answer/id #uuid "bff8657e-afd1-51b1-994d-a0d36032bc77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378185} {:answer/id #uuid "14057f5c-8e34-59bf-9689-6d27ce194bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378186} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3f703e-aa28-5e90-93c4-3f328d98427e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd3f703e-aa28-5e90-93c4-3f328d98427e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378187} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c7c3c39-78bd-5ea4-993e-0621539a534e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3546d6ca-c236-59ab-90e2-5cf833951b0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-103060" :question/problem "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $E?$\n\n- $F∧(F∨E)$\n- $E∧(F∨E)$\n- $E∨(F∨E)$" :question/worked-solution "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nE∧(E∨F)≡E.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $E.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F∧(F∨E) & ≡F≢E.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $E.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E∧(F∨E) & ≡E∧(E∨F) \\\\  & ≡E.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $E.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4106fe70-b9df-5621-ba64-66703855aaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Conjunction Absorbs Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86a82f03-efa1-504e-80f8-4b017713e4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378190} {:answer/id #uuid "0baa6114-af76-53b7-a9c7-022cb452550a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378191} {:answer/id #uuid "e35de28a-6dfc-58db-b2ab-af8b18b12b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378192} {:answer/id #uuid "210f1082-3a14-5e89-8d10-ed7a2c6b1d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378193} {:answer/id #uuid "3c6eb4c1-c4b0-5fdf-9d80-e216c1f6fde6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e35de28a-6dfc-58db-b2ab-af8b18b12b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378192} :answer-field/id #uuid "c332d7e7-5c3e-5d52-9494-b6aeb2c207b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "897313ab-8a44-5c6f-bd61-a0fa12f9c240" :question/math-academy-id "q-103062" :question/problem "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $G?$\n\n- $(H∨G)∧G$\n- $H∧(H∨G)$\n- $(G∨H)∨G$" :question/worked-solution "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nG∧(G∨H)≡G.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $G.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(H∨G)∧G & ≡G∧(H∨G) \\\\  & ≡G∧(G∨H) \\\\  & ≡G.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is *not* logically equivalent to $G.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H∧(H∨G) & ≡H≢G.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $G.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4106fe70-b9df-5621-ba64-66703855aaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Conjunction Absorbs Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8bc9176-817d-55a3-b3fa-f704413af082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378197} {:answer/id #uuid "d9adbd11-9e77-5157-b694-27b2f25e93be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378198} {:answer/id #uuid "a3010ec6-7cf7-5890-98d7-91dcac146296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378199} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ef0211-8b0a-5071-8ea5-9a6cce73c6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378200} {:answer/id #uuid "692e20f0-bb5f-5c4a-a847-26cd93864aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9adbd11-9e77-5157-b694-27b2f25e93be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378198} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a378547-3a39-5ee5-855d-0bb581ff7675" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4d683c7-8d10-55e5-a90e-d913d4664ae0" :question/math-academy-id "q-103067" :question/problem "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $A?$\n\n- $¬B∧(A∨¬B)$\n- $(¬B∨A)∧A$\n- $A∧(¬B∨A)$" :question/worked-solution "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nA∧(A∨¬B)≡A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $A.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬B∧(A∨¬B) & ≡¬B∧(¬B∨A) \\\\  & ≡¬B≢A.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $A.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬B∨A)∧A & ≡A∧(¬B∨A) \\\\  & ≡A∧(A∨¬B) \\\\  & ≡A.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $A.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∧(¬B∨A) & ≡A∧(A∨¬B) \\\\  & ≡A.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4106fe70-b9df-5621-ba64-66703855aaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Conjunction Absorbs Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba3b1751-5953-5fca-8888-36a7a3297a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378204} {:answer/id #uuid "f02ab0f2-8c8d-5fd8-963f-d04a3c8c656b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378205} {:answer/id #uuid "0feae2e8-2525-5ecf-8d7d-7e68e9b38b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378206} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9e9ac1-3a4c-54fa-9933-143e3aab6af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378207} {:answer/id #uuid "debc70be-b144-586f-87cd-0bea5ee97e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba3b1751-5953-5fca-8888-36a7a3297a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378204} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f67a62a-6a40-51ca-bcf5-0c92f198f397" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7d8581e-cdcd-55c5-92e2-f687cc9d64c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-103068" :question/problem "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $C?$\n\n- $¬D∧(C∧¬D)$\n- $C∧(¬D∨C)$\n- $(¬D∨C)∧C$" :question/worked-solution "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nC∧(C∨¬D)≡C.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $C.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C∧(¬D∨C) & ≡C∧(C∨¬D) \\\\  & ≡C.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬D∨C)∧C & ≡C∧(¬D∨C) \\\\  & ≡C∧(C∨¬D) \\\\  & ≡C.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4ea352-2a82-5ff5-8c08-b7f9a09929e6" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Disjunction Absorbs Conjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83ba6d6a-ce85-5d7a-ac83-99813ff625ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378211} {:answer/id #uuid "feb3043d-57de-5fe3-b7fe-bc46f17b5857" :answer/ty