igned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4ea352-2a82-5ff5-8c08-b7f9a09929e6" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Disjunction Absorbs Conjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be3f373f-097e-5973-b701-d5b004c0fd53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378218} {:answer/id #uuid "70dc7167-be75-5b59-9080-653b2e08d444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378219} {:answer/id #uuid "112df888-f091-5999-b6e7-093fb47c02cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378220} {:answer/id #uuid "3e50ea63-ccfd-5b82-a88f-3ec1d14257b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378221} {:answer/id #uuid "ab633a40-ba15-55a8-8e21-728caf9da3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e50ea63-ccfd-5b82-a88f-3ec1d14257b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378221} :answer-field/id #uuid "13896387-fb89-55ff-9543-c77a05e2ccbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ccdaee72-7ba3-5cab-ba35-f5476892e5d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-103319" :question/problem "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $¬D?$\n\n- $¬D∨(C∧¬D)$\n- $¬D∨(¬D∧C)$\n- $(¬D∧C)∧¬D$" :question/worked-solution "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\n¬D∨(¬D∧C)≡¬D.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $¬D.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬D∨(C∧¬D) & ≡¬D∨(¬D∧C) \\\\  & ≡¬D.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $¬D.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬D∨(¬D∧C) & ≡¬D.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $¬D.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4ea352-2a82-5ff5-8c08-b7f9a09929e6" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Disjunction Absorbs Conjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf44c45-6264-5067-b969-338239b49b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378225} {:answer/id #uuid "4e49342a-ca7e-5775-9f4a-e41bf83697aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378226} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd54191-d76e-5439-9a85-e598cd859f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378227} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee6730c-ecaf-5d44-9122-f26e2c8f40eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378228} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6a1f88-1f4d-5b57-8a93-e48e03a9c29f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cf44c45-6264-5067-b969-338239b49b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378225} :answer-field/id #uuid "b753ef60-f718-50ef-820c-2bf38adabb02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3034ba1-2c82-5776-b118-ddbefff303ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-103320" :question/problem "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $L?$\n\n- $L∨(¬M∧L)$\n- $(L∧¬M)∨L$\n- $(¬M∧L)∨L$" :question/worked-solution "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nL∨(L∧¬M)≡L.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $L.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L∨(¬M∧L) & ≡L∨(L∧¬M) \\\\  & ≡L.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $L.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(L∧¬M)∨L & ≡L∨(L∧¬M) \\\\  & ≡L.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $L.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬M∧L)∨L & ≡L∨(¬M∧L) \\\\  & ≡L∨(L∧¬M) \\\\  & ≡L.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129087 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07b63c0c-daaa-5f5b-acb3-58d6f36ee869" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b368f458-efb8-544f-9c52-9be536fdf394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378232} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d4fe7e-3216-55b5-9dfd-bcb24f8e9506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378233} {:answer/id #uuid "15efd6a1-4757-5e0a-b81d-6837a9b8f7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378234} {:answer/id #uuid "bf04cbe6-254f-54ee-83ab-c807b88b5c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378235} {:answer/id #uuid "52db4d93-3096-5b70-ba79-90c05387f7b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52db4d93-3096-5b70-ba79-90c05387f7b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378236} :answer-field/id #uuid "c12ca190-d978-55a0-af55-963098af9ae7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "957e916a-1a46-5f09-b0b4-71f6f746df0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104666" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca165438c64994306acd6031d5f8e5a0e6d4d0d11a5f6fea8097b19f5105438c.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- It has a removable discontinuity at $x=3$\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=3$\n- It is continuous at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(3)=-1$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, II is true, and I and III are false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52fbd0d4-0de5-5f72-9254-f4e6e749d8bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378273} {:answer/id #uuid "cd232d85-718b-55dd-b3bd-4959c54b256d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378274} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9c45da-2cca-5062-859c-ef15e5286a9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378275} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fc7e25-ff19-52ea-a6b5-23084f8b905e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378276} {:answer/id #uuid "f1040cc9-4da3-5149-8fd2-2917139a6e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1040cc9-4da3-5149-8fd2-2917139a6e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378277} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a3582bb-94c4-57db-bf7b-5789d14fe916" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c082af6c-4b35-55f0-b925-7876243430ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-105443" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }3x+y<8,\\,0<x<3}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0\\le x<6,\\,2<y\\le 4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}<6}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,3).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf78b597-2e0d-5ac3-a402-98015c0fc8ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378280} {:answer/id #uuid "95a53f72-241f-505f-b555-9dfaa3cc979e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378281} {:answer/id #uuid "5543a50c-b2ff-5dfd-bade-f2b59c0333f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378282} {:answer/id #uuid "f93fc1aa-6b0e-53ed-bc89-2205e4c596be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378283} {:answer/id #uuid "58915450-0f4d-587c-b49b-a676b7487737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95a53f72-241f-505f-b555-9dfaa3cc979e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378281} :answer-field/id #uuid "983b98b3-1d62-5314-ae8b-eaf7a34ae81a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f4b0cdf-9fe1-57d6-a009-4edf0331d401" :question/math-academy-id "q-105444" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,6\\le x<9,\\,0<y<2}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}<3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(6,1).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(8,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The third set is also *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d3501b-96ef-5fbd-9d93-788021156ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c74eb4-0458-5c07-a1be-ce22a222467f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378329} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb2e554-2202-5241-b4c2-e51f96043697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378330} {:answer/id #uuid "0738f027-f1c3-5b6d-92cb-1dc8a383dfd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378331} {:answer/id #uuid "f985d338-31f6-5c85-a29d-d4f6ffc65f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378332} {:answer/id #uuid "aa07e9a6-d7ae-5f85-a82b-6b8c807a6520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0738f027-f1c3-5b6d-92cb-1dc8a383dfd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378331} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb46f8f-f33d-57ce-85bf-52b4e690d538" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4da29991-8f92-54a5-9016-f20e2647e29f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106802" :question/problem "$\\,$ **If** you need an umbrella, **then** the sky is not clear.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\n- If the sky is clear, then you do not need an umbrella.\n- If you do not need an umbrella, then the sky is clear.\n- If the sky is not clear, then you need an umbrella." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/0d/0deb19675402bf931111bb80cb616682aac08491b5cd3e1ab5812da05b6df8a8.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive.\n\n- Statement I is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement III is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9518d52-cb17-52af-828c-ca2d05c1f181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378350} {:answer/id #uuid "a2941d64-aa24-576d-bc80-dd9f6ed90e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378351} {:answer/id #uuid "08afe76d-888c-511f-8820-1ac6afb5ba4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378352} {:answer/id #uuid "4b936b56-fcb2-5307-a485-2bf8793efcf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378353} {:answer/id #uuid "0fdc3f67-6458-5e56-8339-e91b6cb6f3b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2941d64-aa24-576d-bc80-dd9f6ed90e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378351} :answer-field/id #uuid "45dc1888-563d-57e2-87c8-eacef8e3e71e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29e169b1-20c5-5789-9178-14e4b85ea9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-107117" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $S$ containing $2\\times 2$ matrices that are not invertible. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $S$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $S$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $S$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $S$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ then\n${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ each contain a row consisting only of zeros, so they are not invertible. However,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis invertible. So, $S$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, if $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix that is not invertible then $det(A)=0$ and therefore for all $\\alpha \\in R$ \n\n$$\ndet(\\alpha A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot det(A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $S$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $S$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec283456-8469-54e5-9273-6368155316ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378357} {:answer/id #uuid "864d553e-fd54-5137-9109-7f382e7fc8ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378358} {:answer/id #uuid "6736ccec-e6d4-5ed4-9fd2-f42fe1d4525e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378359} {:answer/id #uuid "e27cc063-b6a0-5ef8-a8eb-afe67e1565fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378360} {:answer/id #uuid "207bf73a-882c-5f28-8d6a-ac6e5dde111f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e27cc063-b6a0-5ef8-a8eb-afe67e1565fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378360} :answer-field/id #uuid "53f9552d-e64e-5f3e-ab12-e1050c060f72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff663474-9982-50d0-b983-22c1b4e4d38b" :question/math-academy-id "q-107144" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $B={f\\in C[0,1]\\,|\\,\\,f(x)\\le 1\\,\\,∀x\\in [0,1]}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under function addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if  $f(x)=g(x)=1$ for all $x\\in [0,1]$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =f(x)+g(x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in [0,1].$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example,  $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in [0,1]$ and take and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(x) & =2f(x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in [0,1].$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5ec2df1-9695-5260-92e7-c7b1c9bbe2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378364} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4b50ec-445e-5ae3-a632-454aedbc08a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378365} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf82d2f-eac8-511a-a9f7-6b4b3e5010a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378366} {:answer/id #uuid "4c763b96-de5a-5310-9329-8ba319084f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378367} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba41388-e322-5bd5-a6d0-1ff7f8297cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ba41388-e322-5bd5-a6d0-1ff7f8297cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378368} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a4ed511-2c7d-526a-afbb-83f70ff9c8fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecd1a414-f091-5cfd-86f0-d0c82f6d9564" :question/math-academy-id "q-107147" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $K$ containing all nonconstant functions.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $K$ is closed under function addition\n- $K$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $K$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $K$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\n*Hint: $f$ is a constant function if $f(x)=\\text{constant}$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, the functions $f(x)=x+2$ and $f(x)=-x-1$ are in $K$, however\n\n$$\n(f+g)(x)=1\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in [0,1]$ and hence $f+g$ is a constant function. \nSo, $K$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, the product of the scalar zero by any function is the constant function zero. So, $K$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $K$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124614 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Stochastic Vectors and Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0240ee35-bf1a-518d-b734-22e7fc2595b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378673} {:answer/id #uuid "c102312d-4f06-5ee7-bfba-19a3e85466e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378674} {:answer/id #uuid "608e0fb6-5eed-5bfe-b2ff-5a12f96ee232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U,$ $V,$ and $W$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378675} {:answer/id #uuid "d00e77ed-9e7d-55d5-8191-e5d0ee50ec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378676} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fcd242-22e8-5d68-97fe-c0929b9c3ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c102312d-4f06-5ee7-bfba-19a3e85466e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378674} :answer-field/id #uuid "470f887c-a240-5a37-9fd2-2eaded721c71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90365b47-9514-5b9b-bb0b-216780f54607" :question/math-academy-id "q-135487" :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.8\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $U$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $V$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.1+0.9=1 \\\\ 0.2+0.8=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $W$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are non-negative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.5\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.5+0+0.5=1 \\\\ 0+1+0=1 \\\\ 0.5+0+0.5=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$V$ and $W$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd3c14e6-cdac-5b50-83d2-c7a7e936d3c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378852} {:answer/id #uuid "7e056cb0-ca61-54f2-a5e9-73842a82ebde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378853} {:answer/id #uuid "c9cb356d-0a4a-57bd-9dbf-4365785bf3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378854} {:answer/id #uuid "d6fe1489-c5d9-51b7-a695-df2f0bfed8ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378855} {:answer/id #uuid "301c3c2d-1689-5485-a99c-8b0d727eadbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd3c14e6-cdac-5b50-83d2-c7a7e936d3c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378852} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fce85d8-31bd-5894-a084-f12d40765a2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7cfd8147-c3e0-5cab-9b14-cee4034ac307" :question/math-academy-id "q-161623" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c2b4e3d11f16920ce24a279289808d3f5dc4e68563525f1417c8767a5446b461.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A=A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb417859-755d-5aba-86aa-b3d5222c2b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378859} {:answer/id #uuid "40b7a775-b680-565a-a722-54389de635d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378860} {:answer/id #uuid "74d00351-7635-5bcc-958e-85d902d007a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378861} {:answer/id #uuid "4f0e71e2-ad4e-53b3-a2df-6e11f13cda76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378862} {:answer/id #uuid "e83bf7f2-5a83-5bde-bf2a-1bbec5b4c606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb417859-755d-5aba-86aa-b3d5222c2b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378859} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5b297f9-60ed-5e8b-8034-bab5354f7b95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28c149d9-63e5-5d01-abf9-5e8569624b8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161639" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<2,\\,0<y<x}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce9cac70-f327-5d5a-b159-03990d1597d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378866} {:answer/id #uuid "7bfeac63-b679-5d58-bf44-9d5e881c362e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378867} {:answer/id #uuid "bbbd8c42-6354-58f7-acdb-41558eacab16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378868} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d36bd0-54b6-5fec-868a-f1737080c195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378869} {:answer/id #uuid "116f96ba-73bd-5ef8-91cb-ca3e555ae3c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "116f96ba-73bd-5ef8-91cb-ca3e555ae3c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378870} :answer-field/id #uuid "39d81623-879e-5aa1-aff5-e89fdb5f84b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be4d8952-950b-5fb0-a574-0337f64e1d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-161648" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are open?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0<x<3,\\,0<y<6-2x}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }1<x<4,\\,x<y<{x}^{2}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le {y}^{2}\\le 5}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3224235d-c85f-581e-b0a5-fa619bf3e2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378873} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec09210-3416-5440-a20c-8e780f2effa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378874} {:answer/id #uuid "2180dbea-78a1-57c5-87db-f486a6914b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378875} {:answer/id #uuid "3257f157-5b66-55fe-830a-873790844a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378876} {:answer/id #uuid "7789b753-b1d3-5748-ae54-82d9e789e198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ec09210-3416-5440-a20c-8e780f2effa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378874} :answer-field/id #uuid "6419e7cc-8024-5bbd-a920-bed8d2a11d14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6e963f0b-a2c7-5ae0-844a-f3e405d79aa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161649" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-x\\ge {y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cup ∂A=A$\n- $A⊂\\text{int}A$\n- $A-\\text{int}A=∂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/88/88f35519cf1aa01bcc9e4fb91b24c2bc2a1a823867ec2670563b0b45f2643a0a.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/64/649c33f1738a35f3d298038bd2cf05d3b435424a4efbbb1e6143cf0d56b2bfd6.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cup ∂A=A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $A⊈\\text{int}A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-\\text{int}A=∂A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eda1965-d655-5550-9bb5-13e7a9c1b406" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statements Expressed Using Symbols"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0884726-4451-5d71-83c1-d9be50d60dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378990} {:answer/id #uuid "21e54d15-7bff-5813-81ad-492fd0ce5723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378991} {:answer/id #uuid "789e15a1-7716-5ac1-9f75-f5213e4da2ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378992} {:answer/id #uuid "c36afdc8-cbfe-582d-a25a-9b2cd80e52e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378993} {:answer/id #uuid "40a3ccf2-ac93-55df-9628-c028ee8d1eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21e54d15-7bff-5813-81ad-492fd0ce5723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378991} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a61c89-518a-5b23-95a0-52cf19d8dd98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66e732f7-06d5-591e-98a5-b6812f6bf5f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-174419" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n\n- $P:(-2)\\cdot 3$\n\n- $Q:5>3$\n\n- $R:7\\in N$" :question/worked-solution "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options:\n\n- $P$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\n- $Q$ is a statement. This statement is *true* because $5$ is larger than  $3.$\n\n- $R$ is a statement. This statement is *true* because $7$ is a natural number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ and $R$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4954fe5-f1fe-590a-9540-280a14606a49" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a20f8869-7a4b-52c9-baef-b0f4212a05de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379136} {:answer/id #uuid "fba68979-6c10-588e-9822-61d04d45d0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379137} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5f71cb-00d9-58b2-90d7-99c0332c1d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379138} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8fd27e-9cdc-57f0-94c4-1c1df1dc1533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379139} {:answer/id #uuid "eded1fba-3349-5c34-970c-22f48833ba96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fba68979-6c10-588e-9822-61d04d45d0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379137} :answer-field/id #uuid "284af02a-4845-5f36-932b-3d57f135e5e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c162f27f-023e-512b-91b9-54632834b487" :question/math-academy-id "q-182238" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are finite?\n\n- $P({R,e})$\n- $P({x\\in R∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0})$\n- $P({(-1)^{n}∣n\\in Z})$" :question/worked-solution "The power set of a finite set is finite, while the power set of an infinite set is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Set I is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\nP({R,e})={∅,\\,{R},\\,{e},\\,{R,e}}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $4.$\n\n- Set II is infinite since the underlying set ${x\\in R∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0}$  is infinite.\n\n- Set III is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\n{(-1)^{n}∣n\\in Z}={-1,1},\n$$\n\nand, as a result, \n\n$$\nP({(-1)^{n}∣n\\in Z})=P({-1,1})={∅,\\,{-1},\\,{1},\\,{-1,1}}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4954fe5-f1fe-590a-9540-280a14606a49" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dd5f945-cd7f-5486-b1c5-ae14eb219277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379143} {:answer/id #uuid "a501a546-2f3a-5850-a577-41016260c3db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379144} {:answer/id #uuid "97a7214c-7191-5613-a2bc-8dddea9596d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379145} {:answer/id #uuid "7388f416-1adf-51b9-9ac1-d49299548e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379146} {:answer/id #uuid "77c634f1-f581-52e7-acd9-a57e23e8f9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a501a546-2f3a-5850-a577-41016260c3db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379144} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aab6c6d-5ceb-5786-ac10-901f1b223f35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d277777-ae06-5b12-9091-65c43e7bf489" :question/math-academy-id "q-182241" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are finite?\n\n- $P({Z,t,n})$\n- $P(Z)$\n- $P({{2}^{n}∣n\\in Z})$" :question/worked-solution "The power set of a finite set is finite, while the power set of an infinite set is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Set I is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\nP({Z,t,n})={∅,\\,{Z},\\,{t},\\,{n},\\,{Z,t},{Z,n},\\,{t,n},\\,{Z,t,n}}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $8.$\n\n- Set II is infinite since the underlying set $Z$ is infinite.\n\n- Set III is infinite since the underlying set ${{2}^{n}∣n\\in Z}$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a731d645-8f61-5883-8199-a661bf2002f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379176} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6b4fc0-a7e9-5aea-8f4e-cf195f6ab4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379177} {:answer/id #uuid "52d5cb9c-e0f0-5cb4-9fc5-333c92252584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379178} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7be0f8-1bda-5fd5-a3c8-cbcededcd943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379179} {:answer/id #uuid "a599cd85-e18d-5cc7-88c6-a748c9d524ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52d5cb9c-e0f0-5cb4-9fc5-333c92252584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379178} :answer-field/id #uuid "58c03c95-18a8-5627-a540-087d874d704c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b9dc8cb9-65d8-59f4-a25c-a9f9b514997d" :question/math-academy-id "q-182505" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $¬P⇒Q≡P∨¬Q$\n- $¬(¬P⇒Q)≡¬P∧Q$\n- $¬(¬P⇒Q)≡¬P∧¬Q$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $¬P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬(¬P)∨Q,$ which in turn is equivalent to $P∨Q.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n¬P⇒Q≡P∨Q\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(¬P⇒Q)≡¬(P∨Q).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(P∨Q)≡¬P∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(¬P⇒Q)$ is equivalent to $¬P∧¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d3501b-96ef-5fbd-9d93-788021156ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "800ab06c-2ece-526e-a123-6e7779005898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379311} {:answer/id #uuid "304d6701-6994-5dac-a09d-7d00a8a65603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379312} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ea9514-459e-5aba-aa45-4d9052848394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379313} {:answer/id #uuid "465df49a-7838-5b37-bdbc-1c7183d158de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379314} {:answer/id #uuid "56b7a178-0432-56df-a8b5-e0bfec95706e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56b7a178-0432-56df-a8b5-e0bfec95706e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379315} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbfc8154-2a5f-5e44-afde-5561582686a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d0274f7-9e96-5c91-af2a-50bf61bf0ba6" :question/math-academy-id "q-183341" :question/problem "$\\,$ **If** $8$ is even **then** $4\\in {2,4,8}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\n- If $4\\in {2,4,8},$ then $8$ is even.\n- If $4\\notin {2,4,8},$ then $8$ is not even.\n- If $8$ is not even, then $4\\notin {2,4,8}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/0d/0deb19675402bf931111bb80cb616682aac08491b5cd3e1ab5812da05b6df8a8.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive of the given statement.\n\n- Statement I is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement III is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11f20d51-8248-5571-8f98-e7ef675035c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379413} {:answer/id #uuid "cd02aaba-65be-57fc-b02b-376ab809d02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379414} {:answer/id #uuid "9a994f4d-6048-5ada-b244-da4a6b872e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379415} {:answer/id #uuid "0acbe4f0-2ed3-52c2-8a9e-d3a38412a195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379416} {:answer/id #uuid "a2dd1279-326a-5987-a55b-decc309c855f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0acbe4f0-2ed3-52c2-8a9e-d3a38412a195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379416} :answer-field/id #uuid "160e5d13-0f83-577a-91ea-265646d1a7fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec98e74e-e68c-5045-a599-d3ec1260eaf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-330299" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=1\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{{x}_{1}(t)-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dce4286-552d-5198-8e60-ae77cf3ff5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379420} {:answer/id #uuid "95a5e686-1269-5d81-a1dd-9ff2c45dcc71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379421} {:answer/id #uuid "2186a39d-4c02-5910-b7fb-1213d1919bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379422} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbd7686-2587-593e-9a8d-997b6f64f602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379423} {:answer/id #uuid "ab32738a-ed25-573a-a522-733662acefce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2186a39d-4c02-5910-b7fb-1213d1919bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379422} :answer-field/id #uuid "3917585f-6220-5ca1-94fd-e86e8a7709e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "028e3468-5c29-5e67-a28b-97b2d534ac0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-330306" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-6\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+9\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab933be-d8a6-5168-9229-b4aab6bedb49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379606} {:answer/id #uuid "d89e1c21-d1dd-5f33-9785-95e68c85465d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379607} {:answer/id #uuid "2d24ec4f-680a-5f70-9d7a-4e483d0a76f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379608} {:answer/id #uuid "557af1e3-b08c-5247-96f6-e932bfb7528f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379609} {:answer/id #uuid "730e8ed3-e6f3-5a4f-8416-43d28c8e6b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "557af1e3-b08c-5247-96f6-e932bfb7528f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379609} :answer-field/id #uuid "08acca3e-bb84-5168-9c50-67de5dec3326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff653b13-8b67-5277-ad4c-9f98277f418a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342282" :question/problem "$$\nx(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-2){y}^{′}+4y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-2){y}^{′}+4y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-2}{x(x+3)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{4}{x(x+3)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-2}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{4}{x(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=0,$ since both denominators at $x=0$ equal zero. So, $x=0$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38b816ba-2958-5c3c-8cd7-2b88d0cc5920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379613} {:answer/id #uuid "02b1e940-5c01-5474-b7d5-62d2e4fc6518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379614} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4937b-aaa8-53f1-a955-cb8c02b1dbd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379615} {:answer/id #uuid "363726e9-9c96-50d2-9d88-ee2838c287e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379616} {:answer/id #uuid "43839927-d2ed-5af2-947f-97ba41db0d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02b1e940-5c01-5474-b7d5-62d2e4fc6518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379614} :answer-field/id #uuid "200e14ff-e4d1-57ae-9ea3-19076e82d0d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "39331851-2b44-5819-96f4-7d54c8cd9bd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-342366" :question/problem "$$\n(x+3){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{3}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}$ are analytic about $x=-3.$\n- The functions $(x+3)P(x)$ and $(x+3)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-3.$\n- The point $x=-3$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{3}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x+3}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-3,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-3.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+3)P(x)=(x+3)\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}\n$$\n\n is *not* analytic about $x=-3$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-3.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=-3$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=-3$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f126da-5f3b-5af8-9aee-d6f0a4c61c49" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Shape of a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa93218-27b0-5ad5-8222-b7e37e2e20e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379768} {:answer/id #uuid "870c2e4a-2968-5ad3-9de4-98a3ab8a3888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379769} {:answer/id #uuid "3e07e993-4d1b-5f3e-bc88-fb33e5cf4d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379770} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7d4122-ae51-5da7-9538-4f3a5a4e7279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379771} {:answer/id #uuid "67511d28-193a-5f98-b1ad-09961ee6f34d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e07e993-4d1b-5f3e-bc88-fb33e5cf4d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379770} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e2961e2-c4d5-5918-ab70-c6f6a98c5129" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "036af2f8-8d3f-5a9f-9714-c21bb558dc15" :question/math-academy-id "q-35538" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=4x-3y-1$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=-1$ gives a line\n- $c=0$ gives the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$\n- $c=2$ gives a parabola" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3y-1 & =c \\\\ 4x-3y-(c+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{c+1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1$ then the level curve is $y=\\frac{4}{3}x,$ which is a line.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $y=\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{1}{3},$ which is a line different to the axes $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curve is $y=\\frac{4}{3}x-1,$ which is not a parabola.\n\nSo, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f126da-5f3b-5af8-9aee-d6f0a4c61c49" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Shape of a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b971f02-4dda-55b8-a905-b2c02dc4ccd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379905} {:answer/id #uuid "666233fa-03e9-5d3f-9041-88cf94599702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379906} {:answer/id #uuid "40ff7db2-1368-5950-8bc5-efe42e64ccf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379907} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8e1c50-ad71-5b07-b586-a803f3ecc2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379908} {:answer/id #uuid "5d03f4b2-8f1e-55e5-9224-e865497962c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f8e1c50-ad71-5b07-b586-a803f3ecc2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379908} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cfafc86-a646-52b6-ac85-752f96950f81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3aaf864-2fae-5bdc-a851-6e2a3303364c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71398" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2y}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=0$ gives a circle\n- $c=1$ gives a hyperbola\n- $c=2$ gives a parabola" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2y} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2y & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ which is not a circle.\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is $y=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ which is not a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curve is $y=2-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ which is a parabola.\n\nSo, only statement III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3149834-ef92-5016-a84a-40b207fee519" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9e78cd0-978d-506e-868e-8f83ca2997fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379947} {:answer/id #uuid "37dec8a5-f8e6-5cc7-8e9a-857e6b978568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379948} {:answer/id #uuid "043e74e4-44a8-5f9f-a9d4-625b37cab07b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379949} {:answer/id #uuid "f95e0e68-6456-5561-b1dc-7e93d3d74f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379950} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ce9216-f20f-5028-a7db-a11d12ae2903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "043e74e4-44a8-5f9f-a9d4-625b37cab07b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379949} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a2667ff-2890-55a8-93a7-c69f02f7fbdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4218ace-9c46-5876-8096-9bc751d57412" :question/math-academy-id "q-71793" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- $[-2,2]$\n- $[3,9]$\n- $[-1,1)\\cup (3,4]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $[-2,2]$ and $[3,9]$ are closed because:\n\n- The boundary points of $[-2,2]$ are $-2$, and $2.$ Both of them lie in the set.\n\n- The boundary points of $[3,9]$ are $3$, and $9.$ Both of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other set is not closed because $[-1,1)\\cup (3,4]$ has a boundary point $1$, which does not lie in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3149834-ef92-5016-a84a-40b207fee519" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a6df2c-1d53-5764-ac8c-3952f99881e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379954} {:answer/id #uuid "263d6be3-09b1-550c-ba66-f9becdcc13fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379955} {:answer/id #uuid "aaef3ba8-a8a8-50b1-86d1-ccf87710b7e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379956} {:answer/id #uuid "81d4b7e2-7934-5a13-8dd9-6fdef4f24124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379957} {:answer/id #uuid "33047e0b-d4b1-5ed8-9908-c4b35a6c199a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33047e0b-d4b1-5ed8-9908-c4b35a6c199a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379958} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d20cdf6-6afd-5c88-b3e2-2ec66158dd68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c4a042d-b417-5a76-bd5b-ca2066d08ead" :question/math-academy-id "q-71795" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- $(-3,1)$\n- $[0,2]\\cup [6,7]$\n- $(-1,3)\\cup (4,7]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $[0,2]\\cup [6,7]$ is closed. It consists of two parts, where the boundary points are $0,2,6,$ and $7.$ All of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other sets are not closed because:\n\n- $(-3,1)$ has a boundary point $1$, which does not lie in the set.\n\n- $(-1,3)\\cup (4,7]$ has a boundary point $3$, which does not lie in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3149834-ef92-5016-a84a-40b207fee519" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5f1dd57-43e7-5134-89fd-b1f1e3038ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379961} {:answer/id #uuid "c4feb077-a267-505c-831a-200f40aea191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379962} {:answer/id #uuid "714f89d8-1d11-59c9-ad7e-699414828708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379963} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d7b5a7-c514-559c-bac4-f3da5f896b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379964} {:answer/id #uuid "4033cbd4-eb1f-58a8-b330-03e076419290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5f1dd57-43e7-5134-89fd-b1f1e3038ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379961} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9fc4fee-b20d-58a2-80e0-8f8db607a662" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9b6f2b8-c18f-5a12-b634-f89ac5f5481f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71796" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- $(6,10]$\n- $[2,5]$\n- $[0,1]\\cup [3,4]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $[2,5]$ and $[0,1]\\cup [3,4]$ are closed because:\n\n- The boundary points of $[2,5]$ are $2$ and $5.$ Both of them lie in the set.\n\n- The boundary points of $[0,1]\\cup [3,4]$ are $0,1,3,$ and $4.$ All of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other set is not closed because $(6,10]$ has a boundary point $6$, which does not lie in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3149834-ef92-5016-a84a-40b207fee519" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d6fc105-4665-5793-840a-d38f2e07c65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379968} {:answer/id #uuid "3e75ea62-d875-500e-b00a-ad8139208c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379969} {:answer/id #uuid "f27839c1-6f71-56a5-b057-e5734e69d496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379970} {:answer/id #uuid "f06b782e-2708-5653-9046-5d9e5df40d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379971} {:answer/id #uuid "e7817c5e-15e4-5414-b3d3-9f16dd15eb81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f27839c1-6f71-56a5-b057-e5734e69d496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379970} :answer-field/id #uuid "531480b0-2b5e-5d39-b602-bae56ec77aeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0cdf362-49b4-5e81-91f4-944c2a137d99" :question/math-academy-id "q-71801" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are open?\n\n- $(-2,1]\\cup [3,9)$\n- $[4,6)\\cup (9,11)$\n- $(-1,4)\\cup (6,7)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $(-1,4)\\cup (6,7)$ is open. It consists of two parts, where the boundary points are $-1$, $4$, $6$, and $7.$ None of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other sets are not open because:\n\n- $(-2,1]\\cup [3,9)$ has four boundary points $-2$, $1$, $3$, and $9$, and the points $1$ and $3$ lie in the set.\n\n- $[4,6)\\cup (9,11)$ has four boundary points $4$, $6$, $9$, and $11$, and the point $4$ lies in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "588a0299-39d2-5e67-8345-4096dc183a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380064} {:answer/id #uuid "2577b5bd-3a9f-586b-9df0-209646aaa8f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380065} {:answer/id #uuid "25e6d69d-16a1-5171-91bd-140824c8cb0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380066} {:answer/id #uuid "a667b049-bb99-5839-85c7-c744fa78e2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380067} {:answer/id #uuid "6749e05f-0872-50cb-bb57-234783af8974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6749e05f-0872-50cb-bb57-234783af8974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380068} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffb8a45f-9e44-5c61-a1a3-31531e8a62dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb1f3bd1-4155-5e86-902f-cd91483f7d31" :question/math-academy-id "q-74963" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $1$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{({x}^{2})}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4106fe70-b9df-5621-ba64-66703855aaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Conjunction Absorbs Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad534fa4-8ecb-5a96-9a22-d961abac53d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380161} {:answer/id #uuid "175fd012-5329-594f-b94a-7957331392c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380162} {:answer/id #uuid "47a273a3-cc46-5403-b048-3acac53389d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380163} {:answer/id #uuid "41295324-c55f-59bc-b94d-194855cfbcf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380164} {:answer/id #uuid "babde9ba-09d7-5111-80d3-515bf6a20def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad534fa4-8ecb-5a96-9a22-d961abac53d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380161} :answer-field/id #uuid "f90a2622-3760-5f6e-95ef-3cc4e8794630" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1b738de-914d-5dd9-b0ba-d383cdcec6e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-81718" :question/problem "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $Q?$\n\n- $¬R∧(Q∧¬R)$\n- $Q∧(¬R∨Q)$\n- $(¬R∨Q)∧Q$" :question/worked-solution "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nQ∧(Q∨¬R)≡Q.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $Q.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $Q.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q∧(¬R∨Q) & ≡Q∧(Q∨¬R) \\\\  & ≡Q.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $Q.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬R∨Q)∧Q & ≡Q∧(Q∨¬R) \\\\  & ≡Q.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4ea352-2a82-5ff5-8c08-b7f9a09929e6" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Disjunction Absorbs Conjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5a03801-6e31-5f12-9230-03a73941cd5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380168} {:answer/id #uuid "f543ff52-14d3-564d-97fa-276917dadb38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380169} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe548d7-442b-5e51-bc64-b15e8bc2960d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380170} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c7f146-02c7-56f7-b44f-9d7216074c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380171} {:answer/id #uuid "3665fcb6-129f-59bf-91af-779ad97764da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3665fcb6-129f-59bf-91af-779ad97764da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380172} :answer-field/id #uuid "77cbc687-c9f8-5de6-95b7-7080149d026a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34117327-fd37-5989-b657-502be019389b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81721" :question/problem "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $P?$\n\n- $P∨(Q∧P)$\n- $(Q∧P)∨P$\n- $P∨(Q∨P)$" :question/worked-solution "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nP∨(P∧Q)≡P.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $P.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∨(Q∧P) & ≡P∨(P∧Q) \\\\  & ≡P.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $P.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Q∧P)∨P & ≡P∨(Q∧P) \\\\  & ≡P∨(P∧Q) \\\\  & ≡P.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $P.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4ea352-2a82-5ff5-8c08-b7f9a09929e6" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Disjunction Absorbs Conjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f4ce61-3577-53d7-963d-36460b2e90f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380175} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba38e16-d4fd-5b3f-be09-5278fb466686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380176} {:answer/id #uuid "73dd5019-d63b-574e-8ab2-ef6118f6019f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380177} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd78e84-32e2-534a-98df-660adfb45fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380178} {:answer/id #uuid "406955b2-095c-5f0b-86eb-9d7fd8469fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29f4ce61-3577-53d7-963d-36460b2e90f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380175} :answer-field/id #uuid "3495e208-31b8-5c35-b62f-cb23f17cccc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c524db33-b009-501a-aa7b-2de183456b8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81722" :question/problem "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $M?$\n\n- $M∨(N∨M)$\n- $(M∧N)∨M$\n- $M∨(N∧M)$" :question/worked-solution "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nM∨(M∧N)≡M.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $M.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(M∧N)∨M & ≡M∨(M∧N) \\\\  & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M∨(N∧M) & ≡M∨(M∧N) \\\\  & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d3501b-96ef-5fbd-9d93-788021156ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4d18692-acd4-59c1-9e3e-3325f2a1d8bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380216} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb47751-4190-594d-8962-b1bdeabfd734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380217} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff976d3-f9fc-5777-a49c-cca37cb97b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380218} {:answer/id #uuid "700d45aa-06ce-5c29-863a-7b2ad7864c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380219} {:answer/id #uuid "de279420-643a-5713-8ec6-4038075b3edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ff976d3-f9fc-5777-a49c-cca37cb97b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380218} :answer-field/id #uuid "a33330e2-9b6d-5e27-b1a3-9b9123c05f62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b3cb87d-282a-5d16-9402-d3db5936c8ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-82660" :question/problem "$\\,$ **If** I am not sleeping, **then** I am awake.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\n- If I am not awake, then I am sleeping.\n- If I am sleeping, then I am not awake.\n- If I am awake, then I am not sleeping." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/0d/0deb19675402bf931111bb80cb616682aac08491b5cd3e1ab5812da05b6df8a8.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive.\n\n- Statement I is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement III is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d3501b-96ef-5fbd-9d93-788021156ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac381034-e631-549e-bad1-e6ad09bfa423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380223} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc83512-b6ce-5cf4-ae34-46b41380d0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380224} {:answer/id #uuid "674ae613-75b3-5494-bfed-ba479c8015be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380225} {:answer/id #uuid "25f6399b-33da-5e56-93c5-770a003aa88d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380226} {:answer/id #uuid "0f7cead4-5bad-5177-b792-cbf3cc8b7481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "674ae613-75b3-5494-bfed-ba479c8015be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380225} :answer-field/id #uuid "2efbc5e9-e76e-5fc3-896f-36357157ae30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b3c08af-1740-5428-a4c7-1f507e3193d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-82664" :question/problem "$\\,$ **If** we do not go to the cinema, **then** we watch a movie at home.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\n- If we do not watch a movie at home, then we go to the cinema.\n- If we watch a movie at home, then we do not go to the cinema.\n- If we go to the cinema, then we do not watch a movie at home." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/0d/0deb19675402bf931111bb80cb616682aac08491b5cd3e1ab5812da05b6df8a8.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive.\n\n- Statement I is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement I is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement III is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c14137a6-cad1-5298-b5a2-c139b65ffae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380286} {:answer/id #uuid "705418e3-f85f-51c4-ad54-8a6c193ce8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380287} {:answer/id #uuid "51745566-4e78-5087-968c-d41d22ca8950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380288} {:answer/id #uuid "8625bbbd-05fa-5dfc-8255-b4d000e4a7fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380289} {:answer/id #uuid "30571e39-5e28-50ec-b2d4-01c8adb78af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51745566-4e78-5087-968c-d41d22ca8950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380288} :answer-field/id #uuid "5064ba53-0659-5afe-a7f4-717377b374e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e8696fa-a30e-5b2e-a6f9-20152471933a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94621" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $H$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $H$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $H$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $H$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $H$ such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $0,$ then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}\n$$\n\n is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to \n\n$$\n0+0=0.\n$$\n\n This matrix belongs to $H.$ So, $H$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A$ is an element of $H$ such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $0,$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\nkA\n$$\n\n is a matrix such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to \n\n$$\n0\\cdot k=0.\n$$\n\n This matrix belongs to $H.$ So, $H$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $H$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79d4ec7e-d11b-5ec7-8c41-58fc348cc2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380293} {:answer/id #uuid "9e852983-e617-589f-9345-3d1a5ddd35c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380294} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5d255a-d3bd-566b-98c1-097719fce02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380295} {:answer/id #uuid "18cd04a4-ec8a-5a96-9cc3-fb88c2b8b05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380296} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcbc443-f97c-5a55-bcd1-41b9d52e7f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79d4ec7e-d11b-5ec7-8c41-58fc348cc2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380293} :answer-field/id #uuid "4832a433-2062-5a14-93ce-7c582a8f7b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02488e4e-8845-5c5e-a156-a562e8b67a01" :question/math-academy-id "q-94627" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $D={[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a<0}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $D,$ then  ${a}_{1}<0$ and ${a}_{2}<0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n is a matrix from $D,$ since ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}<0.$ So, $D$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example,  if $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=-1,$ then $A$ is an element of $D$ and $k\\in R,$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis *not* in  $D.$ So, $D$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $D$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64521aa0-de87-5b2a-92ea-e716d1ededef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380327} {:answer/id #uuid "8a17211f-f42c-5be4-a84c-f85cf65bbafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380328} {:answer/id #uuid "8f80af38-2c29-5c8f-9141-359c445e8659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380329} {:answer/id #uuid "1d64f238-a82f-5e38-a9c1-02e3ec78f8e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380330} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe51de3-b474-5722-8561-97d3baa24356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64521aa0-de87-5b2a-92ea-e716d1ededef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380327} :answer-field/id #uuid "788bc413-f1ef-5c02-b581-c04dd78a4cbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f94c3089-795c-58b5-a020-124bd7b98fb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97859" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ non-invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $B?$\n\n- ${\\lambda }^{3}-4\\lambda =0$\n- ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +4=0$\n- $\\lambda (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -1)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is non-invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ must be an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $B$ since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a2de38-a526-51be-85dc-9d3ad9bcdf84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380340} {:answer/id #uuid "4db29910-7cd7-5d2e-ab75-c202ca721e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380341} {:answer/id #uuid "036076bf-321f-5de0-8688-b4ed7b720620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380342} {:answer/id #uuid "3180fd57-4d23-5261-afe9-a98997a0c7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380343} {:answer/id #uuid "03a3e027-308a-54a1-b639-04029d0cfaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17a2de38-a526-51be-85dc-9d3ad9bcdf84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380340} :answer-field/id #uuid "db73dc06-a582-5ddd-9457-adb5f0ec282d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cf92ef9-ce4a-5cbb-9e9d-9e8a2989e53d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97944" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(B+2I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B+2I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B+2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=B+2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "517a26c9-93c0-528e-9ebb-fdd9d2e4dcc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380347} {:answer/id #uuid "e000068d-935c-5e7d-8e8f-885cc53f9188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380348} {:answer/id #uuid "92cbc27d-acb5-519c-bb8c-f0728dbe096a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380349} {:answer/id #uuid "82a0b4ae-88f7-5acb-ae78-a3ccb08ab4cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380350} {:answer/id #uuid "1b442d88-edbf-51cb-8a82-1d42923aef42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "517a26c9-93c0-528e-9ebb-fdd9d2e4dcc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380347} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c816ec-65dd-5615-b3c7-bf32ebc9d583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f636785f-61ec-504d-b842-394a5a79199b" :question/math-academy-id "q-97947" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(C-2I)x=0$ has only zero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n- $\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-2I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040843fa-3262-5284-9689-6210d333e76f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380354} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3cc701-fb34-571d-991a-e4e4efd3665c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380355} {:answer/id #uuid "6861f0ae-1c41-5653-be66-42922aa52f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380356} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6ae954-608c-5d50-80eb-c77483f1c86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380357} {:answer/id #uuid "127ea8e0-aa09-59ee-9f0b-cacbf872d638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d3cc701-fb34-571d-991a-e4e4efd3665c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380355} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b719da3-f1cb-5c61-b0be-164da8e55f23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a33206bb-7969-5524-a745-290fffeff16f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97967" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B-2I)=3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)=1$\n- $B-2I$ is non-singular\n- $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B-2I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B-2I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B-2I$ is singular, then $(B-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337518a9-39a1-57e3-8686-74a135f7c3d7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Oriented Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e8c03da-a978-575f-8c1b-085775eedfd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380388} {:answer/id #uuid "d52b304e-f41f-506e-8c46-a26ba751bac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380389} {:answer/id #uuid "2d81a262-18e3-5166-8bdf-031b1be1ba78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380390} {:answer/id #uuid "512102b8-ad65-5fff-a52e-dd66cd9fdd50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380391} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c67f71-ae2b-5910-924c-2151951d0f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d81a262-18e3-5166-8bdf-031b1be1ba78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380390} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b52052c-ee18-5917-807e-849961b875e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f035c68d-f32c-5e67-98cc-62c8475830d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-98341" :question/problem "![](images/05/05e7d61ebc2cc2b3d36915d448f6d9a6498376b858f72294f2ffc9342af38a7a.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?" :question/worked-solution "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is not closed.\n\n- Curve II is not simple.\n\n- Curve III is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve IV is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c83ba197-baf1-5a47-b9ea-ad671205f039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380395} {:answer/id #uuid "460f8ed1-22bf-558b-8f1d-b4d2c002aa5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380396} {:answer/id #uuid "c602afc3-785d-515a-b903-b1afc4adfb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380397} {:answer/id #uuid "decb6e79-8728-54fc-9a0b-c9dd44e09e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380398} {:answer/id #uuid "20946bc2-8448-5c07-b5d7-0292baab0c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c602afc3-785d-515a-b903-b1afc4adfb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380397} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdd30202-889c-59ba-bb4d-be2188ca8246" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb972e63-3e46-543b-9a40-0120a6183656" :question/math-academy-id "q-100120" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t-4,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-4,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1}+(2t-4){|}_{2}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)-4)\\cdot (1-1)+(2(2)-4)\\cdot (1-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2-t,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1}+(2-t){|}_{2}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-1)+(2-2)\\cdot (1-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-4,2-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-t){|}_{1}+(2t-4){|}_{2}\\cdot (2-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)-4)\\cdot (2-1)+(2(2)-4)\\cdot (2-2) \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51c38ba3-947f-5e44-8a81-230ba3328424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380408} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bcb700-18f0-56e2-9cae-eb30dc7e16d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380409} {:answer/id #uuid "e1048327-e452-5193-9750-011189b52b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380410} {:answer/id #uuid "d056adf6-c365-548e-bc8e-b0eccd6d5134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380411} {:answer/id #uuid "06234a79-3f8c-59b1-87c1-abec77e9f5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1048327-e452-5193-9750-011189b52b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380410} :answer-field/id #uuid "f53b0d47-d591-5204-a1db-1c099fe1fb90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39aa7418-1a3f-5a9e-9642-6c49cf6dc36a" :question/math-academy-id "q-100129" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t+2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+2,t-2⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t-2){|}_{-1}+(2t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t-2){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(-1)+2)\\cdot ((-1)-2)+(2(2)+2)\\cdot (2-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{2} \\\\  & =((-1)-2)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(2-2)\\cdot (2+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+2,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(2t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(-1)+2)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(2(2)+2)\\cdot (2+1) \\\\  & =18\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152132 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe686b5-c7f0-58e6-9e46-e769a9193b21" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9edddf5-258f-5460-be41-769be7be7493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380415} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb47ec2-c757-5886-84b4-5cdcfa876408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380416} {:answer/id #uuid "4e2596b0-d234-56e7-8728-709792caafff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380417} {:answer/id #uuid "b0557b9b-6a95-5397-8bd1-73204b720540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380418} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab2a9cd-07b7-53a6-9e95-221c351ccc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9edddf5-258f-5460-be41-769be7be7493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380415} :answer-field/id #uuid "f68eb7eb-0775-5899-889d-14aa509971ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db140378-9b82-5420-9a09-f186b3b4c9b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-104956" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d5a71c78a8e8aa5c4c17189a7e2fcf739af2b4e6d93819e87aa9f3f7135c8cd.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- There exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=4$\n- $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a surjection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(1)=4$ (we have an arrow from $1\\in X$ to $4\\in Y$). So, there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152132 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe686b5-c7f0-58e6-9e46-e769a9193b21" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f46e921c-8d10-5a86-850d-07aca450b07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380422} {:answer/id #uuid "6c34f9b1-4b80-511c-aad7-e6080c9270e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380423} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6e8b91-24c1-5c2a-ac18-b9f2c154c502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380424} {:answer/id #uuid "da9eedf4-c1a4-5c97-a3f1-9ac23e093e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380425} {:answer/id #uuid "35d288ec-e2d8-50c4-ba5f-879ac5859967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da9eedf4-c1a4-5c97-a3f1-9ac23e093e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380425} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec2b7b45-79b4-5ca1-8ad1-d6a00cb89e1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7e09caa-cacf-54dd-ae93-0599cdfc3d85" :question/math-academy-id "q-104957" :question/problem "![](images/80/801bb98497bc275acda854447fffee30721bc54b2711ac632c2269f0c1eb64ed.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- There exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=-1$\n- $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a surjection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As we can see from the diagram, there are no elements contained within $X$ that have an arrow to $-1\\in Y.$ Hence, $f(x)\\ne -1$ for all $x\\in X.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is false,  it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152132 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe686b5-c7f0-58e6-9e46-e769a9193b21" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4567e1b-2d8d-57d3-847c-17a7d6d27a7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380429} {:answer/id #uuid "b8bb58f2-782e-534c-b1d7-ecece5c0a897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380430} {:answer/id #uuid "7b04098a-04d9-513d-9435-926d7c7a843a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380431} {:answer/id #uuid "2e76a177-045a-5eae-a0b6-bbb0f7cca672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380432} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6114ff-6f94-5845-b624-0a89f8899ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8bb58f2-782e-534c-b1d7-ecece5c0a897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380430} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab975203-0009-5284-9e61-11b2ba863295" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65240711-5d62-532c-a650-6596f72fcd4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104959" :question/problem "![](images/94/94f14d4a1876588cbfb2abc4a1542d5961587745a6e24aeae270d15f75f834db.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- There exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=7$\n- $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is **onto**" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(1)=7$ (we have an arrow from $2\\in X$ to $7\\in Y$). So, there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=7.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider $y=5.$ As we can see from the diagram, there are no elements contained within $X$ that have an arrow to $5\\in Y.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection (also known as *onto*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152133 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63a2b195-d0b3-580c-9230-58533d86afd5" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Domain and Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fea23d2-363a-57c8-82a0-74159786da57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380436} {:answer/id #uuid "bb9d68c7-8aae-5030-aeee-be7bbf76aaaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380437} {:answer/id #uuid "03327d8a-67bc-51b0-aad8-0860846d9ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380438} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9b7a1e-758c-53b4-8062-27c9d4872fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380439} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe13655-59b0-5bd0-aecc-9fb4816f1048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fea23d2-363a-57c8-82a0-74159786da57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380436} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4a8656d-1a39-53f0-8a58-a8098512d0fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32986725-0ef3-5392-8fb4-f9776573a8e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104963" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-1)^{3}?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[1,\\infty )$\n- $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is **onto**" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function. \n\n![](images/80/80630fae7c4f1e29d855e9a68917156ccf3ecbefd0efc7bda5a9bd0e903c90db.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph covers all $y$-values, so the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$, then for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1324fd0b-5388-5133-ad6a-72d658e3c903" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "020bc159-a9f5-505e-bb3b-c01fa9984734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380443} {:answer/id #uuid "a381baa8-d91d-5926-b7dc-35746f35bf38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380444} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d954bf-5e79-5fe6-9c9e-e6dbd08b6094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380445} {:answer/id #uuid "609608a1-4ac9-531c-a5d5-8cc2f76ba843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380446} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cae79b-7afa-5ab6-af23-94c4f116816f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "609608a1-4ac9-531c-a5d5-8cc2f76ba843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380446} :answer-field/id #uuid "71b0d4e9-4a30-506c-9105-a0dca9dce546" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f86e164-e19a-5fdd-bd77-619a454eb721" :question/math-academy-id "q-104973" :question/problem "![](images/94/94c9daf5f16776eef520e40f68a38dc5e05c4ceb5470cdc18880ac288a9ce031.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-1,5]\\to [-1,4]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[-1,3]$\n- $∀\\,y\\in [-1,3],$ $∃\\,x\\in [-1,5]$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a surjection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $3$ and the lowest $y$-value is $-1.$ The graph covers all $y$-values between $-1$ and $3$, including these numbers, so the range of the function is $[-1,3].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of $f$ is $[-1,3]$ and $[-1,3]$ is contained in that range, it follows that for all $y$ contained within $[-1,3],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[-1,5]$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=4.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $[-1,4],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[-1,5]$ such that $f(x)=y.$ In conclusion, $f$ is not a surjection of $[-1,5]$ onto $[-1,4].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1324fd0b-5388-5133-ad6a-72d658e3c903" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7169a561-b8f0-5fd8-b2cb-07ec134a6809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380450} {:answer/id #uuid "40bade29-1cbf-562b-8c33-4284af85c23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380451} {:answer/id #uuid "0660c625-e54f-57ba-963b-bf51d1356163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380452} {:answer/id #uuid "4e75d7f4-955b-5fdd-8214-5153e94aefa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380453} {:answer/id #uuid "21e9aee4-8a84-5a8a-a120-f5629772fbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0660c625-e54f-57ba-963b-bf51d1356163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380452} :answer-field/id #uuid "f39635f8-50f8-55eb-b399-984c785839dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f729bcf8-f875-5e33-9fc0-12814c32c7be" :question/math-academy-id "q-104981" :question/problem "![](images/91/916c7fa803c4fb2949cfc9a7f1c8fd05dde8cfe004673eb22f313daeb89083be.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-1,4]\\to [-1,4]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[0,4]$\n- $∀\\,y\\in [-1,4],$ $∃\\,x\\in [-1,4]$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a surjection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $4$ and the lowest $y$-value is $0.$ The graph covers all $y$-values between $0$ and $4$, including these numbers, so the range of the function is $[0,4].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider, for example,  $y=-1.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x\\in [-1,4]$ such that $f(x)=-1.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $[-1,4],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[-1,4]$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $[-1,4]$ onto $[-1,4].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1324fd0b-5388-5133-ad6a-72d658e3c903" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91c5fbc1-6ecb-532e-8397-389621499ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380457} {:answer/id #uuid "bc903d16-f754-55d5-8f07-a3dd22923a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380458} {:answer/id #uuid "b4270df1-5e8d-54b9-9d32-d0afb9b670ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380459} {:answer/id #uuid "d821f9e7-16d3-506f-9826-6778ed252995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380460} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f818f9-5f25-5378-af54-b62fb97db3cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4270df1-5e8d-54b9-9d32-d0afb9b670ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380459} :answer-field/id #uuid "565d897e-4780-5b32-be50-8238066938f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18cf57da-d5c7-5b11-9220-be1574e018d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-104983" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to [-2,2]$ which is given by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+1?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[0,2]$\n- $∀\\,y\\in [-2,2],$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is **onto**" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/e1/e1a02621cfacc791c1b100b01957195f1f41e9bf78b65286eabbd13363930c67.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $2$ and the lowest $y$-value is $0.$ The graph covers all $y$-values between $0$ and $2$, including these numbers, so the range of the function is $[0,2].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider, for example,  $y=-1.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $[-2,2],$ there exists some $x$ contained in $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $R$ onto $[-2,2].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6fa67074-d3c8-5000-95a3-7f935936ab7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surjections For Functions Defined Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5541a274-04c4-5faf-aeb1-c991d5425bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380464} {:answer/id #uuid "932934dc-d872-5e4f-b3d1-18854f187b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380465} {:answer/id #uuid "49d22d10-770d-51ab-9ecf-b3a111c042b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380466} {:answer/id #uuid "fd002e28-352a-5116-860f-f50a2ccff8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380467} {:answer/id #uuid "0544a742-cd76-59cc-8090-f17e5cb913d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0544a742-cd76-59cc-8090-f17e5cb913d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380468} :answer-field/id #uuid "04eacd6c-e1ff-5301-b9fb-365c92fc68d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15ff4cc7-7dbb-588f-ad41-efc18bba219b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104997" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\n- $g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\sqrt{x}$\n- $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=-{x}^{3}$\n- $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{2}+x$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose ${2}^{1/4}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)={2}^{1/4}.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)=\\sqrt{x}={2}^{1/4}$ is $x=\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- II is a surjection. Given $y\\in R,$ choose $x=-\\sqrt[3]{y}\\in R.$ Then $h(x)=h(-\\sqrt[3]{y})=-{(-\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3}=y.$\n\n- III is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=-1.$ In fact ${x}^{2}+x>0$ for all $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6fa67074-d3c8-5000-95a3-7f935936ab7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surjections For Functions Defined Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a3447a-047f-53a1-a1a7-14d0160e224a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380471} {:answer/id #uuid "295e3c83-1294-5bab-8afc-57ca26687db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380472} {:answer/id #uuid "990ea436-8870-5993-9e5a-3ba7ac5e05ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380473} {:answer/id #uuid "d67a9d77-88b8-5a9d-ba13-abf64c83c4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380474} {:answer/id #uuid "01ed7d59-dc74-5781-b41f-6241c43c513b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "990ea436-8870-5993-9e5a-3ba7ac5e05ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380473} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a3f5143-83c3-5718-baa2-eb554713414f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "170d4c2a-1e31-530e-82ce-91551c575c42" :question/math-academy-id "q-104999" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\n- $f:{-1,0,1,2}\\to {-1,0,1,2,3}$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{2}-1$\n- $g:(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n- $h:[0,\\infty )\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)=\\sqrt{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in {-1,0,1,2,3}.$ There is no number $x\\in {-1,0,1,2}$ such that $f(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $f(x)={x}^{2}-1=2$ is $x=\\sqrt{3},$ but $\\sqrt{3}\\notin {-1,0,1,2}.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ such that $g(x)=2.$ In fact, $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1$ for all $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- III is a surjection. Given $y\\in [0,\\infty ),$ choose $x={y}^{2}\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Then $h(x)=h({y}^{2})=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}}=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6fa67074-d3c8-5000-95a3-7f935936ab7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surjections For Functions Defined Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78bf5df9-2155-5aaf-90d9-2cd85159ec9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380478} {:answer/id #uuid "3122d473-a840-5c91-9362-bdc5d5e4bb35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380479} {:answer/id #uuid "8636bde8-51b4-59ab-805e-d878f16182c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380480} {:answer/id #uuid "98bc1a46-8c35-54ea-9e57-4b780508ce5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380481} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f89fad-3a88-5eed-afb6-77a91c9e2a42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78bf5df9-2155-5aaf-90d9-2cd85159ec9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380478} :answer-field/id #uuid "527f27db-963b-508b-a409-e2056490114b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d8dd107-bc43-5af5-9ea8-482dedecfe3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105006" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\n- $f:{1,2,4,8}\\to {-1,0,1,2,3}$ defined by $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡x$\n- $g:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡x$\n- $h:{-2,-1,0,1,2}\\to {0,1,2}$ defined by $h(x)=|x|$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1$ from the range. The only number $x$ that satisfies $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡x=-1$ is $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ but $\\frac{1}{2}\\notin {1,2,4,8}.$\n\n- II is a surjection. Given $y\\in R,$ choose $x={10}^{y}\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Then $g(x)=g({10}^{y})=\\operatorname{log}⁡{10}^{y}=y.$\n\n- III is a surjection. Indeed, $h(-2)=2,$ $h(-1)=1$ and $h(0)=0.$ So, for every $y\\in {0,1,2},$ there exists an element $x\\in {-2,-1,0,1,2}$ such that $h(x)=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c805415f-c87d-50c5-b668-948fc46bcc7e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1a49b3f-0c1b-5bf5-af26-635c4bba54eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380485} {:answer/id #uuid "89bf2227-54e9-5c00-9047-413e50c4ff7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380486} {:answer/id #uuid "adfff9aa-358d-53a6-a580-53e56780f751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380487} {:answer/id #uuid "1481a818-97ac-5252-a440-b3d085b28a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380488} {:answer/id #uuid "e073e3db-87a6-56be-bbf2-0031cd0d22c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89bf2227-54e9-5c00-9047-413e50c4ff7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380486} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b80601b-1df0-56b4-a753-a199b40afe68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b06d538-7443-54c1-b503-59c054bf5bb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105118" :question/problem "![](images/26/26ec4cb2ec851e6a1b6d7f0ad99b0a49bf36f95d3c110d3777ca8e4a92177057.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(1)=f(4)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is not an injection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(1)=f(4)=4$ (we have arrows from $1\\in X$ and $4\\in X$ to $4\\in Y$).\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(1)=f(4)$ but $1\\ne 4.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c805415f-c87d-50c5-b668-948fc46bcc7e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23227591-0dbd-51cf-8b68-96d742de06ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380492} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1187ca-2a33-5ef0-8421-7b83ac228ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380493} {:answer/id #uuid "48a97d1e-f9d6-5591-86e4-d5e4189f2ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380494} {:answer/id #uuid "fc002918-6bc7-5d9f-a7fa-f9b2256fadae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380495} {:answer/id #uuid "647d39d1-87e1-549f-b052-8cd11112b1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc002918-6bc7-5d9f-a7fa-f9b2256fadae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380495} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e0d80f-6572-5bbf-8f4d-571b4f8c97b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4be96f58-f72b-5ffd-a1b3-6817c52a2536" :question/math-academy-id "q-105122" :question/problem "![](images/80/80b80ea901c031439e50f42b34146401c75c65f9947ecca75ee8afda94540465.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(4)\\ne f(8)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is not an injection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the diagram, we have $f(4)=1$ (we have an arrow from $4\\in X$ to $1\\in Y$) and $f(8)=1$ (we have an arrow from $8\\in X$ to $1\\in Y$).\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the diagram, $f(4)=f(8)$ but $4\\ne 8.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c805415f-c87d-50c5-b668-948fc46bcc7e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c78f295e-dd31-5603-8284-84119a96b8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380499} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec7a43d-27ef-57fb-aa9d-886e20643d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380500} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1e62f0-7106-5b5a-a5ee-cb8de224060f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380501} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c29f67-b74d-5b3f-ac97-ea0553d0c6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380502} {:answer/id #uuid "e48756ae-fbcc-50f3-9d13-e9521270ccba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c78f295e-dd31-5603-8284-84119a96b8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380499} :answer-field/id #uuid "078df945-0251-5351-a4d1-ece62f0f470f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ef9f00e-29d5-5bdb-ace6-5ec77b1eb401" :question/math-academy-id "q-105126" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cfee09c9688df45bd12654a1847ab9a2e9a311289e89dc52441abbb239138ed.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(-4)=f(-1)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the diagram, we have $f(-4)=8$ (we have an arrow from $-4\\in X$ to $8\\in Y$) and $f(-1)=0$ (we have an arrow from $-1\\in X$ to $0\\in Y$).\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152196 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a347133c-5e5a-5dd2-89ef-bdd14245ee6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa93f529-50eb-54a0-8c81-2c8bd1c1b7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380506} {:answer/id #uuid "8b205019-1f5a-5aa4-a4ef-2119da75236f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380507} {:answer/id #uuid "747dc62c-6a2c-55c6-a1f7-d7e1e10d696c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380508} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1dcb41-e629-5798-bf64-7569082cca12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380509} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe8a4b8-c750-536a-bf0e-42dc1e166f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b205019-1f5a-5aa4-a4ef-2119da75236f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380507} :answer-field/id #uuid "08268410-05b0-5ac4-986e-7a4efd0c99d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a95fdab7-b3ba-5047-982f-ba6ecacd47b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-105129" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2a60bc27e7227cfe55c03611608e5210ad147d07058028e4ea78b10a96280797.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(-1)=f(1)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is an injection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the graph, we have $f(-1)=1$ and $f(1)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $R$ that map to the same element in $R.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aef522dc-c92a-50a1-9a9e-2b100b865ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380568} {:answer/id #uuid "ba70e4ec-1e50-51e3-804f-62a2ab327c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380569} {:answer/id #uuid "546f5ba8-635f-509c-a718-5d331a77cf43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380570} {:answer/id #uuid "a608c25c-ba30-5892-aeca-656907ef2a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380571} {:answer/id #uuid "e9eab9bd-1c17-5cf0-9670-52fcc5c9c416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9eab9bd-1c17-5cf0-9670-52fcc5c9c416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380572} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c9a254-9720-5f7d-9149-5824dded661a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd0235ca-04db-545b-b3a6-a1ff38871984" :question/math-academy-id "q-109853" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$ for every matrix $B?$\n\n- $\\text{Null}(B)$\n- $\\text{Col}(B)$\n- $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$ is the left null space of $B.$ This consists of vectors that, when pre-multiplied by ${B}^{T},$ result in the zero-vector $0.$\n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by ${B}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${B}^{T}$, that is, each column of $B$, by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the column space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Col}(B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $B$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Linear Transformations in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1943e1-0d88-5823-b9ae-5369c5830d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380617} {:answer/id #uuid "bfaffb18-e66c-59e4-bb72-30ab2783fcfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380618} {:answer/id #uuid "e0dd3037-f513-514d-89f4-e0e361bcd455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380619} {:answer/id #uuid "15face52-6d07-5253-ac82-5453f51df93b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380620} {:answer/id #uuid "a27fe515-66b3-5b4a-9bdf-8daa79ded139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfaffb18-e66c-59e4-bb72-30ab2783fcfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380618} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d91ea36-1869-560c-8a6b-acda11fa747d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d9c3015-ea78-542d-9de3-a1c266f8369d" :question/math-academy-id "q-111793" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨1,1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, The condition $\\text{rank}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, $T$ is surjective and so  $⟨1,1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement II is false. The condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172085 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34c119eb-a2e9-5e7b-aece-194690e3575f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380659} {:answer/id #uuid "f11bcfb0-554a-553b-a2cd-9d076207bb59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380660} {:answer/id #uuid "7d17c850-0945-5f89-a53a-ffaee5e1a53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380661} {:answer/id #uuid "04ec628e-d678-57c6-afb0-df8af8ce90b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380662} {:answer/id #uuid "649bb3e8-6868-569d-a178-c5ab91222bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f11bcfb0-554a-553b-a2cd-9d076207bb59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380660} :answer-field/id #uuid "caef7be4-fa1f-5ecd-8432-0db8e2abf1fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1de1af2b-64e8-50cf-80c9-f414743fe7ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-122641" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$\n- $H(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+t{e}^{t}\\,j$\n- $K(x,y,z)=2x\\,i+y\\,j-3z\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $H(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172085 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16526cbf-eed9-5dc8-b49d-07c80cc53aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380666} {:answer/id #uuid "76cc8d11-9c5e-5b66-857e-79223281043f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380667} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfe71e4-e268-5307-ad87-4f7d22a6a41f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380668} {:answer/id #uuid "85e5e6ca-2257-5462-aa74-7664e109c0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380669} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ffeac8-d5ff-53ee-ad33-6d914cd947c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ffeac8-d5ff-53ee-ad33-6d914cd947c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380670} :answer-field/id #uuid "21763b78-6aa3-55ed-be0a-c14268a02c40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "502cb71c-02d6-5e14-a31b-2cee28a0a5ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-122656" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $M(x,y)=⟨y-{x}^{2},3x+{y}^{2},\\frac{1}{x+y}⟩$\n- $N(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1,1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩$\n- $P(t)=⟨{t}^{3},\\sqrt{1-t}\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $M(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  ${R}^{2}$ to vectors in ${R}^{3}$ (not in ${R}^{2}$).\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $P(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  $R$ \n(not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172085 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d818659d-904f-56ab-a894-ba5c08da4a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380680} {:answer/id #uuid "073a4146-a75e-5400-a120-20b8b4dcbe03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380681} {:answer/id #uuid "25a00a6a-d7e9-5e03-949c-8562fa76683a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380682} {:answer/id #uuid "60870366-832c-58f3-ab71-17ad52ae15cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380683} {:answer/id #uuid "4a72a1c4-87b7-54cc-8c12-34d1ba68b017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60870366-832c-58f3-ab71-17ad52ae15cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380683} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee31fa4-3f78-5e6b-b4f0-0888305b3c27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b352a16-fe6f-5946-bac9-7b334007947a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122675" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $C(x,y)=⟨\\frac{1}{x+y},{x}^{2}{e}^{y}⟩$\n- $D(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}-3,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)⟩$\n- $E(x,y)=⟨x+1,y+2,x-y⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ to vectors in ${R}^{3}$ (not ${R}^{2}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172087 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1241cb-e0b2-5b46-8f65-a3c4c3647e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380694} {:answer/id #uuid "81271eb6-6f13-53c5-8aa9-8681bb11bee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380695} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3f72cb-d68f-5920-a038-03163c1fef91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380696} {:answer/id #uuid "2e773405-32c0-5805-81d2-1ac74b422fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380697} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce4f059-c075-5696-ac0c-73923ae49957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ce4f059-c075-5696-ac0c-73923ae49957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380698} :answer-field/id #uuid "26e476e2-9bb5-503b-95c4-0d2fc7849f6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b17979a9-57b7-5c2d-8065-c277267e307e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122686" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,i+(x+y)^{2}\\,j$\n- $Q(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i-\\frac{x}{y}\\,j+\\frac{1}{z+1}\\,k$\n- $R(t)=\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,i-(3t-1)\\,j+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,z\\ne -1,\\,y\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $R(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172087 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7ef600e-3fa7-5dd9-a9fc-2b417fe03e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380701} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ec70d-19a0-5b3e-bfe1-e05b2b422ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380702} {:answer/id #uuid "75de271f-3b44-5352-b40a-86d6328346d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380703} {:an