ef 17592186380740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82213b75-a5ae-5da4-9897-32dd48e61df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380742} {:answer/id #uuid "8c14d478-1d38-5431-905c-fc5810a27fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380743} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb49c3c-6f34-5c69-bd2f-99edd12b5d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380744} {:answer/id #uuid "daf8aca0-cf96-5262-acc3-32c815cb2206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380745} {:answer/id #uuid "105339bb-6a5f-5f2e-b085-338665cd1a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c14d478-1d38-5431-905c-fc5810a27fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380743} :answer-field/id #uuid "4deded69-ab37-54db-926d-bebc06210e8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bbe1542-19d1-529e-93b1-09c0d710a430" :question/math-academy-id "q-127960" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\n- $A∨B$\n- $¬(¬A∧B)$\n- $A∨¬B$" :question/worked-solution "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∨B\\,$ | $\\,¬(¬A∧B)\\,$ | $\\,A∨¬B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬(¬A∧B)$ and $A∨¬B$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f55ac35-0429-5f53-9c82-8a1dc5de7647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380826} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9afac2-8adc-55bb-aa15-253c9092e751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380827} {:answer/id #uuid "3f36b5a3-f0dd-5bbb-a57f-d1eb09881d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380828} {:answer/id #uuid "86cbd8f9-532d-5e3b-b7f0-ebb4a2d9371d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380829} {:answer/id #uuid "96e3027d-f252-527f-aa78-82d026bf2265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b9afac2-8adc-55bb-aa15-253c9092e751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380827} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b8a2a47-8dea-5c8c-b603-8b38ec11a4f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a85798d-b6a4-58e9-9e7d-5ef7eeb355cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-129664" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=-{x}^{2}+2$ and $y=x.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}({x}^{3}-xy)\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,1]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,1]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/3f/3f071c27bfa281abcaabe63d4d67797ea7b051b931ee7658d2a32a58f80a0bac.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{3}-xy)\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle II does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles I and III cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2cb98b9-07e9-5adf-9ed5-d8b22002b55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380833} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e49d4a-779c-51c8-953f-eb542f8be617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380834} {:answer/id #uuid "5f81f7a9-b58a-5b71-9ad7-73bed790eac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380835} {:answer/id #uuid "d1376ad0-7a8b-5e52-b8e6-f60d7630fb80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380836} {:answer/id #uuid "79cde0a5-394f-5336-a8d6-a4d19d941d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7e49d4a-779c-51c8-953f-eb542f8be617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380834} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d00e358-f7cd-57b0-8aa1-e4fe6d25e944" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07cce28a-e3b6-5366-babf-23b7cf23e641" :question/math-academy-id "q-129784" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c54c756568a5f20d1d82a228c333dc4253a82236060ab2a7aea6260b10612140.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-4,3]\\times [-2,3]$\n- ${∬}_{R}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$\n- ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,4]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4b2bc5c-f10b-5b15-9c53-5b71c501c0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380840} {:answer/id #uuid "550e22a7-8d98-5669-a03f-2617ea80ed7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380841} {:answer/id #uuid "082de418-b4f7-599c-972c-ae9a1fee0643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380842} {:answer/id #uuid "d690c9d1-dc38-5277-823b-6228cf42526e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380843} {:answer/id #uuid "a25a7123-e2cd-58ca-857d-079bc6e0ce4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "550e22a7-8d98-5669-a03f-2617ea80ed7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380841} :answer-field/id #uuid "afa4a9d2-1409-5e7a-9148-465b1c07f58d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d32f310d-7c80-57bd-8ae7-821e89abc170" :question/math-academy-id "q-129901" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2ded513c72c8029b7d2909d2e3b37cbd659c4ba60a7be72c81379626a7a1234.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,2]\\times [-1,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/77/77c8e1b1aacd29c6d69aa3c641c1641f43ff2aa1467dc713d417641f8b76b066.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions II and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function I equals ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb4515cd-9e47-5ff7-b9db-80d4caff3722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380847} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd2179a-393c-5d9d-935b-1e1aabd85a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380848} {:answer/id #uuid "294b19d4-4da4-5a2d-964c-fb0115fffed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380849} {:answer/id #uuid "5d683d66-22db-5f8a-9a0c-c12b9c67e331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380850} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2be26d-1186-5a98-9d33-d6449a993930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d683d66-22db-5f8a-9a0c-c12b9c67e331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380850} :answer-field/id #uuid "e22d5e18-f2d7-5ad6-beaf-4cf2d9f87028" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a021db95-99b8-59d4-be5a-36dd8114ebe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129907" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/38/38c44f897999039f88df2c3e549a1229ec9f9a979bbc83dfe1a5d7279701450d.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $y{e}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $y{e}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(3,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc6ddd1b-3048-5d1f-91e7-875fe340b296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381057} {:answer/id #uuid "5d334c11-359b-5ce5-9c2a-7d356fcb8b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381058} {:answer/id #uuid "17df3516-5fc1-571f-9361-95b28863e220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381059} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5f02b0-9995-5f27-85f7-af62c806fbfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381060} {:answer/id #uuid "b32ecd61-4e1d-5b08-87a6-155a284ee4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5f02b0-9995-5f27-85f7-af62c806fbfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381060} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7b80ece-2d88-5c65-908f-57fe97248a31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25283dae-901a-5291-a7bb-9324644a9dab" :question/math-academy-id "q-132732" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}?$\n\n- The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- ${f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{z}(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{{sin}^{2}⁡z}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{x{sin}^{2}⁡z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{z}$ is also defined at $(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-\\frac{(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{(1){sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ae24cf0-0cb1-5c2b-8fa2-e0e3c36e329e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381148} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf89eed-801b-5556-b955-bf52b374350b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381149} {:answer/id #uuid "0e2a316a-647d-592f-9fa8-91403c24a448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381150} {:answer/id #uuid "27f29d33-3db3-564f-878a-4ad21364207b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381151} {:answer/id #uuid "904f6bfc-d6fd-5dd8-9658-873e35964540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccf89eed-801b-5556-b955-bf52b374350b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381149} :answer-field/id #uuid "aca3ef3e-df8d-58e9-b622-660d772f8a4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1896da03-9822-50e1-8935-797efb12d1b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-157255" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =-3[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement I is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152133 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63a2b195-d0b3-580c-9230-58533d86afd5" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Domain and Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a360de5e-ae41-56c9-b93c-bfbd6c7ce575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381357} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b0cecd-1b06-5f53-8c56-02fe3e4b8ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381358} {:answer/id #uuid "319d1ce7-1228-53a3-bd11-944b6efcdf8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381359} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1d3521-8714-5029-8da6-c1948f6b42fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381360} {:answer/id #uuid "02197b08-8649-583f-a3a6-943376163a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6b0cecd-1b06-5f53-8c56-02fe3e4b8ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381358} :answer-field/id #uuid "79412d0a-92ee-5810-920e-0761cc17c8bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97231f8f-64e5-5ea2-a413-41a730985778" :question/math-academy-id "q-170659" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=(x+1)^{2}-2?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[-2,\\infty )$\n- $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is **onto**" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/9e/9e8457a8caa2f9dbdb627521e23029325f9edda757336a7a5fbbffbda14b4985.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $-2,$ and it occurs at the point $(-1,-2).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values from $y=-2$ and greater, including the number $-2,$ so the range of the function is $[-2,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider $y=-3.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=-3.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6927f03c-aa7a-56ac-8cd0-c036a11aadc9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Value of a Statement Involving Nested Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6661bac9-191c-5925-93f4-bb505689a14f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381456} {:answer/id #uuid "664176c4-a482-595a-b227-7a54d76b9b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381457} {:answer/id #uuid "1965617d-32bf-5547-83e4-c0e97da92b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381458} {:answer/id #uuid "b53663e4-6cf8-5a6a-90e6-f31f8b5a56be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381459} {:answer/id #uuid "419a07f5-be41-53c3-9ccd-2ea02a00f286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6661bac9-191c-5925-93f4-bb505689a14f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381456} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4fae91b-ea7b-504e-9ef4-a13632f1541b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5934422-10d9-59ea-acc1-a33dbe6bcea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-171906" :question/problem "Let $m\\in N$ and $n\\in N.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∃\\,n\\in N,\\,5\\le n.$\n- The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$\n- $∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For every natural $m$ there exists natural $n$ such that $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true since $5\\le n$ for some natural number $n$ (e.g., for $n=6$).\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(m):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nis true for any natural $m.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $n=2.$ Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,2\\le n\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $n=3.$ And so on. In general, it's sufficient to take $n=m+1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀\\,m\\in N\\,(\\,{{∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n}_{⏟}}_{P(m)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $N.$ And, according to statement II, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "209d2bd5-6761-5c94-ba92-88b5074ca51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381674} {:answer/id #uuid "199c8724-565e-5dce-ad7e-bfa2f74b7250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381675} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cd27b5-8f1c-59bc-ba84-fd4c32304501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381676} {:answer/id #uuid "c2374090-35ce-576f-8a84-1f7140d89d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381677} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6cbc35-ea42-58a4-bbec-262e4e83fef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c6cbc35-ea42-58a4-bbec-262e4e83fef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381678} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdc97073-4b1c-5916-9f40-170a84490dd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf3548ae-a137-5c57-8262-82ca0ade3b9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-183356" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $A(x):$ $x\\in {-1,0,3,7}\\cap N.$ $\\,$ $B(x):$ $x\\in {3,7}.$ \n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x)⇔B(x)?$\n\n- $x\\in {-1,0,3,7}\\cap N$ is equivalent to  $x\\in N.$\n- $x\\in {-1,0,3,7}\\cap N$ if and only if $x\\in {3,7}.$\n- If $x\\in {-1,0,3,7}\\cap N$ then $x\\in {3,7},$ or if $x\\in {3,7}$ then $x\\in {-1,0,3,7}\\cap N.$" :question/worked-solution "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $x\\in {-1,0,3,7}\\cap N$ if and only if $x\\in {3,7}.$\n\n- $x\\in {-1,0,3,7}\\cap N$ is equivalent to $x\\in {3,7}.$\n\n- If $x\\in {-1,0,3,7}\\cap N$ then $x\\in {3,7},$ and if $x\\in {3,7}$ then $x\\in {-1,0,3,7}\\cap N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f5e0da-6ff9-54f8-9899-0fcb4faf174b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381681} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba148f2-8aed-5afe-b8f6-2d6df1077df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381682} {:answer/id #uuid "2514634f-1c9a-516d-9f01-6fcf8e8bb2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381683} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6bb8e3-7838-5de4-b233-6ca16778703e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381684} {:answer/id #uuid "d12b87f7-5b3d-5c51-b825-9978e6d5f710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2514634f-1c9a-516d-9f01-6fcf8e8bb2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381683} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8661f64-c8fd-5a5e-8d68-13ad71e9e23d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9aa776da-0143-5b4b-9d08-2751ea2726a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-183357" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $A(x):$ $3\\,|\\,x.$ $\\,$ $B(x):$ $3\\,|\\,(x+15).$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x)⇔B(x)?$\n\n- $3\\,|\\,x$ if and only if $5\\,|x.$\n- If $3\\,|\\,x$ then $3\\,|\\,(x+15),$ or if $3\\,|\\,(x+15)$ then $3\\,|\\,x.$\n- If $3\\,|\\,x$ then $3\\,|\\,(x+15),$ and if $3\\,|\\,(x+15)$ then $3\\,|\\,x.$" :question/worked-solution "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $3\\,|\\,x$ if and only if $3\\,|\\,(x+15).$\n\n- $3\\,|\\,x$ is equivalent to $3\\,|\\,(x+15).$\n\n- If $3\\,|\\,x$ then $3\\,|\\,(x+15),$ and if $3\\,|\\,(x+15)$ then $3\\,|\\,x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8ae1369-b7ee-5c00-a777-3fe6f0aa3a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381716} {:answer/id #uuid "6b391104-ba09-582c-a764-3db08a687474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381717} {:answer/id #uuid "0b8606e8-2d30-5d6c-bbb3-aa8426a3b2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381718} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3e489a-837d-5d9c-8b82-1575c46709a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381719} {:answer/id #uuid "50ed8ad0-0200-5320-9567-1a74e669e541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b391104-ba09-582c-a764-3db08a687474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381717} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aacbb1a-6cc8-56de-a3cf-e8097ea08557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1dd2623-6f10-56c3-88a0-4d1c15254f6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-183501" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(y):$ ${y}^{3}\\ge 0$ and ${y}^{2}<0.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set ${R}_{0}^{+},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is ${R}_{0}^{+}.$\n- $∀y\\in {R}_{0}^{+},\\,P(y).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${y}^{3}\\ge 0$\" is ${R}_{0}^{+},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${y}^{2}<0$\" is $∅.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={R}_{0}^{+}\\cap ∅=∅.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne {R}_{0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ does *not* coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀y\\in R,\\,P(y)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc087c2-923a-5910-a9ef-14c65af62588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381723} {:answer/id #uuid "a82bf02c-c138-52ea-bedf-aa6d9acfc4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381724} {:answer/id #uuid "55d75bb9-2dcc-5444-8dd9-16a2e123e059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381725} {:answer/id #uuid "460a515f-8e30-53e1-b2a3-9c9ed3d0dcba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381726} {:answer/id #uuid "d79c1d50-f77c-5ac2-9649-da9e99566897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cc087c2-923a-5910-a9ef-14c65af62588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381723} :answer-field/id #uuid "513be37b-b67b-5495-aa2e-ed37027d1b39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df50f5a8-2783-58fd-a4cd-90489ec7d8e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-183505" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x>0$ or $x\\le 0.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$\n- $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$x>0$\" is ${Z}^{+},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$x\\le 0$\" is ${Z}_{0}^{-}.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={Z}^{+}\\cup {Z}_{0}^{-}=Z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e559b713-e0dc-5119-9e81-6afae77d2e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381730} {:answer/id #uuid "758e76ec-b605-5ae0-b77f-95c41878a815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381731} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1a2f46-9029-5c04-8b12-dc4bfb605f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381732} {:answer/id #uuid "513a5cc1-c39d-50fa-a204-e97f550b4c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381733} {:answer/id #uuid "7affa7f5-9f6c-568e-883a-be6cabd4d82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a1a2f46-9029-5c04-8b12-dc4bfb605f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381732} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d507c6c-26e9-534f-84d7-d4a455d7ca6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9947189b-8991-5f0c-ad01-e90d4c094369" :question/math-academy-id "q-183507" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(z):$ ${e}^{z}<0$ or ${z}^{4}\\le -1.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $R,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $R.$\n- $∀z\\in R,\\,P(z).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${e}^{z}<0$\" is $∅,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${z}^{4}\\le -1$\" is $∅.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=∅\\cup ∅=∅.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ does *not* coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀z\\in R,\\,P(z)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60816447-8e01-558a-98c7-c7d2ea05adac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381737} {:answer/id #uuid "8916a66d-c643-5c2f-bd17-ce73d117d3b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381738} {:answer/id #uuid "2203b41c-ae30-5a22-86e3-3f23ea03e531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381739} {:answer/id #uuid "ab15b8c9-5c02-528d-bc7c-1c3e1595107c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381740} {:answer/id #uuid "050255d5-55c3-5434-b1fb-5676d408b9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60816447-8e01-558a-98c7-c7d2ea05adac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381737} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bdca389-545f-586b-905f-d9f4acda2d50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53e303c8-88d9-51db-bc34-ac5638b42f29" :question/math-academy-id "q-183511" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(z):$ $\\frac{z}{2}\\in Q⇒(z+1)^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Q,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $Q.$\n- $∀z\\in Q,\\,P(z).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$\\frac{z}{2}\\in Q$\" is $Q,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$(z+1)^{2}\\ge 0$\" is $Q.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{―}{Q}\\cup Q=∅\\cup Q=Q.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is not empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Q.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀z\\in Q,\\,P(z)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1a68cd3-a209-55d3-a3d8-25a77ff1f9b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381751} {:answer/id #uuid "82ca3bb3-266a-5eb8-8810-df05717789a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381752} {:answer/id #uuid "65ae5aff-2f74-53fe-b4a3-84427d4067fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381753} {:answer/id #uuid "055c76e7-59ee-5b3b-bf61-9c2f432cc842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381754} {:answer/id #uuid "97582ec3-dc87-5beb-8eb8-161a6bef315e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65ae5aff-2f74-53fe-b4a3-84427d4067fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381753} :answer-field/id #uuid "0477c06d-a387-55dd-a1ab-ae56b8769dcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79851a4f-18e1-5d49-91ff-0a7efb6d90a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-183549" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(z):$ $\\sqrt{z}<1$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $N.$\n- $∃z\\in N,\\,P(z).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=∅.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne N.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ is empty, the statement $∃z\\in N,\\,P(z)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]
